{"id": "xagency-001", "question": "Do youth ride free on both Yolobus and SacRT?", "provenance": {"source": "SacRT published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\n**Yolobus:** Youth ages 0–18 ride free [doc:yolobus-fares].\n\n**SacRT:** The published policy does not specify a fare-free program for youth ages 0–18. SacRT does offer fare-free transit for students in Transitional Kindergarten through 12th grade through the RydeFreeRT program [doc:sacrt-fares], but the published policy does not state the eligibility criteria or how to enroll. For details on RydeFreeRT, contact your school district or visit RydeFreeRT directly.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sacrt-fares", "yolobus-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Do youth ride free on both Yolobus and SacRT?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>**Yolobus:** Youth ages 0–18 ride free [yolobus-fares].</p><p>**SacRT:** The published policy does not specify a fare-free program for youth ages 0–18. SacRT does offer fare-free transit for students in Transitional Kindergarten through 12th grade through the RydeFreeRT program [sacrt-fares], but the published policy does not state the eligibility criteria or how to enroll. For details on RydeFreeRT, contact your school district or visit RydeFreeRT directly.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SacRT: Fares</li><li>Yolobus: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes", "Our mobile fare app Transit Connect allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus and light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nLearn more at sacrt.com/TransitConnect .", "A Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). A Daily Pass can be purchased individually on board a Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect.", "Our mobile fare app ZipPass allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus or light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nNeed a ticket for your next SacRT Light Rail trip?\nOur fare vending machines located at all SacRT light rail stations are simple to use!\n1. Choose your ticket or pass\n2. Pick your quantity\n3. Complete your payment (cash or card)\n4. Take your ticket\nTap2Ride\nTap your contactless card, mobile device or mobile wallet on Tap2Ride devices on bus and SacRT GO vehicles to pay fare.", "A Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). A Daily Pass can be purchased individually onboard a Yolobus or by using Connect Card or the Transit Connect app.", "SacRT offers fare-free transit for students in Transitional Kindergarten through 12th grade with the RydeFreeRT program and has a partnership with Los Rios and Sacramento State for college students to ride SacRT.\nCalifornia State Workers\nSEIU members have access to free transit through SacRT! Other state employee unions are eligible for transit subsidies to cover most of your commute costs."]}
{"id": "xagency-002", "question": "Is the senior discount age the same on MST and Yolobus?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "No, the senior discount ages differ between the two agencies.\n\n**MST** offers a discount fare for riders **65 years and older** [doc:mst-fares].\n\n**Yolobus** offers a senior discount for riders **62 years and older** [doc:yolobus-fares].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12. For time-sensitive details, confirm directly with each agency.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares", "yolobus-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Is the senior discount age the same on MST and Yolobus?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>No, the senior discount ages differ between the two agencies.</p><p>**MST** offers a discount fare for riders **65 years and older** [mst-fares].</p><p>**Yolobus** offers a senior discount for riders **62 years and older** [yolobus-fares].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12. For time-sensitive details, confirm directly with each agency.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li><li>Yolobus: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes", "Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required, and bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center.\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services.\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Thursday, excluding holidays 9am – 12pm; 1pm – 4pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets, and/or Passes onto Connect Cards", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required as our bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center .\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services:\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Thursday, excluding holidays 9am – 12pm; 1pm – 4pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets and/or Passes onto Connect Cards\nAlternative ticket and pass sales location in Sacramento\nTickets, passes and Connect Card services are also available in Sacramento at the Sacramento Regional Transit District (SacRT) Customer Service and Sales Center, 1225 R St., Sacramento (916) 321-2877\nSingle Ride\nTickets are for a one-way trip. A ticket can be purchased individually on Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect.", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month."]}
{"id": "xagency-003", "question": "What single-ride fares do Yolobus and SacRT charge?", "provenance": {"source": "SacRT published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\n**SacRT Single-Ride Fares:**\n- Regular adult: $1.25 [doc:sacrt-fares]\n- Student (TK–12) discount: $1.25 [doc:sacrt-fares]\n- Light rail single ride tickets are valid for 90 minutes from time of validation on light rail only, or you may pay a 25 cent transfer fee when boarding a bus [doc:sacrt-fares]\n\n**Yolobus Single-Ride Fares:**\n- Regular adult (19–61): Local $2.00, Intercity $2.25, Express $3.25 [doc:yolobus-fares]\n- Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled): Local $1.00, Intercity $1.00, Express $1.50 [doc:yolobus-fares]\n- Youth ages 0–18 ride free on Yolobus [doc:yolobus-fares]\n\nYou can purchase tickets on board, via Connect Card, or through the Transit Connect app [doc:yolobus-purchasing] [doc:sacrt-fares].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sacrt-fares", "yolobus-fares", "yolobus-purchasing"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What single-ride fares do Yolobus and SacRT charge?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>**SacRT Single-Ride Fares:**</p><p>- Regular adult: $1.25 [sacrt-fares]</p><p>- Student (TK–12) discount: $1.25 [sacrt-fares]</p><p>- Light rail single ride tickets are valid for 90 minutes from time of validation on light rail only, or you may pay a 25 cent transfer fee when boarding a bus [sacrt-fares]</p><p>**Yolobus Single-Ride Fares:**</p><p>- Regular adult (19–61): Local $2.00, Intercity $2.25, Express $3.25 [yolobus-fares]</p><p>- Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled): Local $1.00, Intercity $1.00, Express $1.50 [yolobus-fares]</p><p>- Youth ages 0–18 ride free on Yolobus [yolobus-fares]</p><p>You can purchase tickets on board, via Connect Card, or through the Transit Connect app [yolobus-purchasing] [sacrt-fares].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SacRT: Fares</li><li>Yolobus: Fares</li><li>Yolobus: Purchasing a Ticket or Pass</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Single Ride Ticket\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$10.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$20.00\n*Discount (senior, disabled or student) monthly or semi-monthly stickers must be affixed to a valid SacRT photo ID, with the exception of the student stickers, which may be attached to a school issued photo ID.\n**Many students in grades TK through 12, are eligible for free transit passes. Check with your school district for details or visit RydeFreeRT.\nBus\nPassengers are required to pay a basic or discount single fare for each trip or may purchase a daily pass valid for unlimited rides on that day.\nLight Rail\nSingle ride tickets are valid for 90 minutes from time of validation on light rail only, or pay the 25 cent transfer fee when boarding a bus.\nFare Accepted by Other Transit Agencies\nYour SacRT ticket or pass maybe eligible to transfer to and from other transit agencies. For more detailed fare info or a list of SacRT fares accepted by other transit agencies, click here .", "Tickets, passes, and Connect Card services are also available in Sacramento at the Sacramento Regional Transit District (SacRT) Customer Service and Sales Center, 1225 R St., Sacramento (916) 321-2877\nSingle Ride\nTickets are for a one-way trip. A ticket can be purchased individually on Yolobus or by using Connect Card or the Transit Connect app.", "Our mobile fare app Transit Connect allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus and light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nLearn more at sacrt.com/TransitConnect .", "A Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). A Daily Pass can be purchased individually on board a Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect.", "Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes", "A Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). A Daily Pass can be purchased individually onboard a Yolobus or by using Connect Card or the Transit Connect app."]}
{"id": "edge-001", "question": "I'm 62. Do I get the senior discount on MST?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nMST offers a discount fare for people 65 years and older [doc:mst-fares]. The published policy does not specify the age at which the senior discount begins — it states only \"65 years and older.\" At 62, you would not meet that published criterion.\n\nTo confirm whether MST offers any other discount programs you may qualify for, or to ask about exceptions, contact MST directly at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or visit mst.org.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm 62. Do I get the senior discount on MST?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>MST offers a discount fare for people 65 years and older [mst-fares]. The published policy does not specify the age at which the senior discount begins — it states only \"65 years and older.\" At 62, you would not meet that published criterion.</p><p>To confirm whether MST offers any other discount programs you may qualify for, or to ask about exceptions, contact MST directly at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or visit mst.org.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST) is partnering with veterans’ services organizations to select individuals needing transportation services. Selected veterans are provided free MST passes to honor their service to our country. For information email [email protected]\nVeterans Group Travel Training\nMonterey-Salinas Transit (MST) provides free fixed-route travel training to teach interested individuals how to safely and independently ride the MST bus system.\nTravel Training", "MST now accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets. At this time, accepted mobile wallets include: Apple Pay , Google Pay, Samsung Pay , and Fitbit Pay . For more information, visit our Contactless Fare Payment Fast Facts and FAQs .\nCash\nCash is inserted into the farebox. Exact fare is not required. If you do not have exact fare the farebox will issue credit for use on future MST trips."], "expected_facts": ["65"], "pair_id": "pair-edge-001", "is_reference": true, "language": "en"}
{"id": "edge-002", "question": "I'm 62 — can I ride Yolobus at the senior rate?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nYes, Yolobus offers reduced fares for seniors age 62 and older. [doc:yolobus-reduced-fare-id]\n\nTo ride at the senior rate, you must show proper identification when purchasing fares and when boarding. [doc:yolobus-reduced-fare-id] The published policy does not specify which forms of ID are accepted for seniors specifically, but it does state that acceptable proof includes: a SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID, Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal), DMV Driver's License or government-issued ID Card, or any other discount ID issued by another transit agency. [doc:yolobus-fares]\n\nSenior fares on Yolobus are:\n- Local Fare: $1.00\n- Intercity Fare: $1.00\n- Express: $1.50\n- Monthly Pass (all routes): $42.00 [doc:yolobus-fares]\n\nIf you don't already have a Yolobus reduced fare photo ID, you may obtain one by visiting the Yolo Transportation District office at 350 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776 (Mon–Thu, 9:00 AM–Noon, 1:00–4:00 PM) or calling (530) 661-0816. [doc:yolobus-reduced-fare-id]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares", "yolobus-reduced-fare-id"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm 62 — can I ride Yolobus at the senior rate?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>Yes, Yolobus offers reduced fares for seniors age 62 and older. [yolobus-reduced-fare-id]</p><p>To ride at the senior rate, you must show proper identification when purchasing fares and when boarding. [yolobus-reduced-fare-id] The published policy does not specify which forms of ID are accepted for seniors specifically, but it does state that acceptable proof includes: a SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID, Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal), DMV Driver's License or government-issued ID Card, or any other discount ID issued by another transit agency. [yolobus-fares]</p><p>Senior fares on Yolobus are:</p><p>- Local Fare: $1.00</p><p>- Intercity Fare: $1.00</p><p>- Express: $1.50</p><p>- Monthly Pass (all routes): $42.00 [yolobus-fares]</p><p>If you don't already have a Yolobus reduced fare photo ID, you may obtain one by visiting the Yolo Transportation District office at 350 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776 (Mon–Thu, 9:00 AM–Noon, 1:00–4:00 PM) or calling (530) 661-0816. [yolobus-reduced-fare-id]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Yolobus: Fares</li><li>Yolobus: Reduced Fare ID</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes", "Our senior (62+) and disabled riders can take advantage of Yolobus’ reduced fares. To qualify for reduced fares, riders must show proper identification when purchasing fares and to bus operators when boarding.\nQualifying riders may obtain a reduced fare photo ID by visiting the Yolo Transportation District office or SacRT Customer Service & Sales Center:\nYolo Transportation District\n350 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776 (Mon-Thu, 9:00 AM-Noon, 1:00-4:00 PM)\n(530) 661-0816", "BeeLine on-demand microtransit fares are a one-way, flat-fee that is the same for all riders.\nRegular | Seniors (62+)/Disabled & Youth (0-18)\nKnights Landing | $2.00 | $1.50\nWinters | $2.00 | $1.50\nWoodland | $3.00 | $1.50", "Yolobus-only and Sacramento Regional Transit Monthly Passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown, and through the first business day of the following month. Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or on the Transit Connect app, but not onboard a Yolobus.", "A Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). A Daily Pass can be purchased individually onboard a Yolobus or by using Connect Card or the Transit Connect app.", "Yolobus-only and Sacramento Regional Transit Monthly Passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown, and through the first business day of the following month. Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or the Transit Connect mobile app, but not onboard a Yolobus."], "expected_facts": ["62"]}
{"id": "edge-003", "question": "What age counts as a senior on SacRT?", "provenance": {"source": "SacRT published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nAccording to SacRT's published fares, a senior is defined as age 62 and older. Seniors (age 62+) are eligible for discount fares, including a single ride ticket for $1.25, a daily pass for $3.50, and a monthly pass for $50.00. There is also a Super Senior Monthly Pass available for riders age 75 and older for $40.00. [doc:sacrt-fares]\n\nFor details about eligibility documentation or to confirm current policy, contact SacRT directly at sacrt.com or call 511.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sacrt-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What age counts as a senior on SacRT?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>According to SacRT's published fares, a senior is defined as age 62 and older. Seniors (age 62+) are eligible for discount fares, including a single ride ticket for $1.25, a daily pass for $3.50, and a monthly pass for $50.00. There is also a Super Senior Monthly Pass available for riders age 75 and older for $40.00. [sacrt-fares]</p><p>For details about eligibility documentation or to confirm current policy, contact SacRT directly at sacrt.com or call 511.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SacRT: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["All fares are subject to change.\nExact change required only on bus; operators do not carry cash. Light rail station fare vending machines accept both cash/credit or debit. Pre-paid tickets and passes must be validated at the fare vending machines before boarding.\nMonthly passes cover the full month, and semi-monthly passes can be purchased for the 1st – 15th or 16th – end of the month.\nAll passengers must pay basic fare unless eligible for discount fare or free fare. View the fare categories page to see if you qualify for senior, disabled or student fare.\nAge 19-61 - Basic\nSingle Ride Ticket\n$2.50\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$7.00\nSemi-Monthly Pass\n$50.00\nMonthly Pass\n$100.00\nSenior (age 62+) - Discount\nSingle\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$25.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$50.00\nSuper Senior Monthly Pass/Sticker (age 75+)*\n$40.00\nPerson with Disabilities - Discount\nSingle\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$25.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$50.00", "Single Ride Ticket\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$10.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$20.00\n*Discount (senior, disabled or student) monthly or semi-monthly stickers must be affixed to a valid SacRT photo ID, with the exception of the student stickers, which may be attached to a school issued photo ID.\n**Many students in grades TK through 12, are eligible for free transit passes. Check with your school district for details or visit RydeFreeRT.\nBus\nPassengers are required to pay a basic or discount single fare for each trip or may purchase a daily pass valid for unlimited rides on that day.\nLight Rail\nSingle ride tickets are valid for 90 minutes from time of validation on light rail only, or pay the 25 cent transfer fee when boarding a bus.\nFare Accepted by Other Transit Agencies\nYour SacRT ticket or pass maybe eligible to transfer to and from other transit agencies. For more detailed fare info or a list of SacRT fares accepted by other transit agencies, click here .", "SacRT offers fare-free transit for students in Transitional Kindergarten through 12th grade with the RydeFreeRT program and has a partnership with Los Rios and Sacramento State for college students to ride SacRT.\nCalifornia State Workers\nSEIU members have access to free transit through SacRT! Other state employee unions are eligible for transit subsidies to cover most of your commute costs.", "Our mobile fare app ZipPass allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus or light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nNeed a ticket for your next SacRT Light Rail trip?\nOur fare vending machines located at all SacRT light rail stations are simple to use!\n1. Choose your ticket or pass\n2. Pick your quantity\n3. Complete your payment (cash or card)\n4. Take your ticket\nTap2Ride\nTap your contactless card, mobile device or mobile wallet on Tap2Ride devices on bus and SacRT GO vehicles to pay fare.", "Our mobile fare app Transit Connect allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus and light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nLearn more at sacrt.com/TransitConnect .", "SacRT offers a variety of fare options to meet your travel needs.\nImportant Notice for ZipPass Users\nLast day to use passes/tickets: April 30, 2026"], "expected_facts": ["62"]}
{"id": "edge-004", "question": "Is the senior discount age the same on MST and Yolobus?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "No, the senior discount ages differ between the two agencies.\n\n**MST** offers a discount fare for riders **65 years and older** [doc:mst-fares].\n\n**Yolobus** offers a senior discount for riders **62 years and older** [doc:yolobus-fares].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12. For time-sensitive details, confirm directly with each agency.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares", "yolobus-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Is the senior discount age the same on MST and Yolobus?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>No, the senior discount ages differ between the two agencies.</p><p>**MST** offers a discount fare for riders **65 years and older** [mst-fares].</p><p>**Yolobus** offers a senior discount for riders **62 years and older** [yolobus-fares].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12. For time-sensitive details, confirm directly with each agency.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li><li>Yolobus: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes", "Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required, and bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center.\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services.\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Thursday, excluding holidays 9am – 12pm; 1pm – 4pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets, and/or Passes onto Connect Cards", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required as our bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center .\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services:\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Thursday, excluding holidays 9am – 12pm; 1pm – 4pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets and/or Passes onto Connect Cards\nAlternative ticket and pass sales location in Sacramento\nTickets, passes and Connect Card services are also available in Sacramento at the Sacramento Regional Transit District (SacRT) Customer Service and Sales Center, 1225 R St., Sacramento (916) 321-2877\nSingle Ride\nTickets are for a one-way trip. A ticket can be purchased individually on Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect.", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month."], "expected_facts": ["65", "62"]}
{"id": "edge-005", "question": "I'm 75 — does SacRT have anything cheaper than the regular senior pass for me?", "provenance": {"source": "SacRT published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes. Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12, SacRT offers a **Super Senior Monthly Pass/Sticker for age 75+** at **$40.00 per month** [doc:sacrt-fares]. This is cheaper than the regular Senior Monthly Pass/Sticker (age 62+) at $50.00 per month [doc:sacrt-fares].\n\nThe published policy does not specify how you document your age or how to apply for the Super Senior pass — contact SacRT directly at sacrt.com or call their customer service for enrollment details.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sacrt-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm 75 — does SacRT have anything cheaper than the regular senior pass for me?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes. Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12, SacRT offers a **Super Senior Monthly Pass/Sticker for age 75+** at **$40.00 per month** [sacrt-fares]. This is cheaper than the regular Senior Monthly Pass/Sticker (age 62+) at $50.00 per month [sacrt-fares].</p><p>The published policy does not specify how you document your age or how to apply for the Super Senior pass — contact SacRT directly at sacrt.com or call their customer service for enrollment details.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SacRT: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["All fares are subject to change.\nExact change required only on bus; operators do not carry cash. Light rail station fare vending machines accept both cash/credit or debit. Pre-paid tickets and passes must be validated at the fare vending machines before boarding.\nMonthly passes cover the full month, and semi-monthly passes can be purchased for the 1st – 15th or 16th – end of the month.\nAll passengers must pay basic fare unless eligible for discount fare or free fare. View the fare categories page to see if you qualify for senior, disabled or student fare.\nAge 19-61 - Basic\nSingle Ride Ticket\n$2.50\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$7.00\nSemi-Monthly Pass\n$50.00\nMonthly Pass\n$100.00\nSenior (age 62+) - Discount\nSingle\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$25.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$50.00\nSuper Senior Monthly Pass/Sticker (age 75+)*\n$40.00\nPerson with Disabilities - Discount\nSingle\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$25.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$50.00", "Single Ride Ticket\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$10.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$20.00\n*Discount (senior, disabled or student) monthly or semi-monthly stickers must be affixed to a valid SacRT photo ID, with the exception of the student stickers, which may be attached to a school issued photo ID.\n**Many students in grades TK through 12, are eligible for free transit passes. Check with your school district for details or visit RydeFreeRT.\nBus\nPassengers are required to pay a basic or discount single fare for each trip or may purchase a daily pass valid for unlimited rides on that day.\nLight Rail\nSingle ride tickets are valid for 90 minutes from time of validation on light rail only, or pay the 25 cent transfer fee when boarding a bus.\nFare Accepted by Other Transit Agencies\nYour SacRT ticket or pass maybe eligible to transfer to and from other transit agencies. For more detailed fare info or a list of SacRT fares accepted by other transit agencies, click here .", "SacRT offers fare-free transit for students in Transitional Kindergarten through 12th grade with the RydeFreeRT program and has a partnership with Los Rios and Sacramento State for college students to ride SacRT.\nCalifornia State Workers\nSEIU members have access to free transit through SacRT! Other state employee unions are eligible for transit subsidies to cover most of your commute costs.", "Connect Transit Card is the region's electronic transit fare payment system. You can now purchase your transit fare online, never have to carry exact change again AND use the card on more than one agency!\n1. Go through the \"get a card\" process\n2. Create an account and buy your transit fare\n3. Receive an email confirming your order\n4. Your Connect Transit Card arrives in 7-10 Business Days", "Our mobile fare app ZipPass allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus or light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nNeed a ticket for your next SacRT Light Rail trip?\nOur fare vending machines located at all SacRT light rail stations are simple to use!\n1. Choose your ticket or pass\n2. Pick your quantity\n3. Complete your payment (cash or card)\n4. Take your ticket\nTap2Ride\nTap your contactless card, mobile device or mobile wallet on Tap2Ride devices on bus and SacRT GO vehicles to pay fare.", "Our mobile fare app Transit Connect allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus and light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nLearn more at sacrt.com/TransitConnect ."], "expected_facts": ["75", "$40"]}
{"id": "edge-006", "question": "I have a Medi-Cal card. Does that get me the reduced fare on Yolobus?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nNo. According to Yolobus policy, to qualify for reduced fares you must show proper identification when purchasing fares and boarding. The published criteria state that valid proof of identification includes \"SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver's License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency.\" [doc:yolobus-fares]\n\nA Medi-Cal card is explicitly excluded from the list of acceptable documents.\n\nIf you believe you may qualify for reduced fares through another form of identification, you can visit the Yolo Transportation District office at 350 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776 (Mon–Thu, 9:00 AM–Noon, 1:00–4:00 PM) or call (530) 661-0816 to discuss your situation. [doc:yolobus-reduced-fare-id]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares", "yolobus-reduced-fare-id"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I have a Medi-Cal card. Does that get me the reduced fare on Yolobus?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>No. According to Yolobus policy, to qualify for reduced fares you must show proper identification when purchasing fares and boarding. The published criteria state that valid proof of identification includes \"SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver's License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency.\" [yolobus-fares]</p><p>A Medi-Cal card is explicitly excluded from the list of acceptable documents.</p><p>If you believe you may qualify for reduced fares through another form of identification, you can visit the Yolo Transportation District office at 350 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776 (Mon–Thu, 9:00 AM–Noon, 1:00–4:00 PM) or call (530) 661-0816 to discuss your situation. [yolobus-reduced-fare-id]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Yolobus: Fares</li><li>Yolobus: Reduced Fare ID</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required, and bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center.\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services.\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Thursday, excluding holidays 9am – 12pm; 1pm – 4pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets, and/or Passes onto Connect Cards", "Our senior (62+) and disabled riders can take advantage of Yolobus’ reduced fares. To qualify for reduced fares, riders must show proper identification when purchasing fares and to bus operators when boarding.\nQualifying riders may obtain a reduced fare photo ID by visiting the Yolo Transportation District office or SacRT Customer Service & Sales Center:\nYolo Transportation District\n350 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776 (Mon-Thu, 9:00 AM-Noon, 1:00-4:00 PM)\n(530) 661-0816", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required as our bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center .\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services:\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Thursday, excluding holidays 9am – 12pm; 1pm – 4pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets and/or Passes onto Connect Cards\nAlternative ticket and pass sales location in Sacramento\nTickets, passes and Connect Card services are also available in Sacramento at the Sacramento Regional Transit District (SacRT) Customer Service and Sales Center, 1225 R St., Sacramento (916) 321-2877\nSingle Ride\nTickets are for a one-way trip. A ticket can be purchased individually on Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect.", "ADA-eligible riders can use ADA paratransit (“Yolobus Special”) service. The Yolobus Special fares vary by distance (local vs. intercity).\nADA Paratransit (\"Yolobus Special\") Products\nADA Paratransit Local Fare | $4.00\nADA Paratransit Intercity Fare | $4.50\nADA Paratransit Premium Fare* | $6.00\n* Premium service is available to medical facilities only in portions of Sacramento and Vacaville that are beyond ¾ miles of Yolobus fixed routes. Destinations within ¾ mile of a Yolobus fixed route are charged the Local or Intercity fare, whichever is applicable\nHow to purchase a ticket or a pass\nMore information on how to purchase a ticket or a pass is available here", "Riders have many choices when paying their fares. While fares can be paid onboard the bus with cash, contactless payment is more efficient and reduces bus boarding times—getting riders to their destinations faster. Contactless fare payment options include the Connect Card and the Transit Connect mobile app."], "expected_facts": ["Medicare"]}
{"id": "edge-007", "question": "I'm 50 but I have a Medicare card. Can I get the MST discount fare?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nYes. MST offers a discount fare for Medicare Card holders [doc:mst-fares]. To use the discount, you will need to present your Medicare Card as proof of eligibility when boarding [doc:mst-fares].\n\nYou can also link your Medicare discount to a contactless bank card through Cal-ITP Benefits for faster verification on each trip [doc:mst-fares-benefits].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares", "mst-fares-benefits"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm 50 but I have a Medicare card. Can I get the MST discount fare?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>Yes. MST offers a discount fare for Medicare Card holders [mst-fares]. To use the discount, you will need to present your Medicare Card as proof of eligibility when boarding [mst-fares].</p><p>You can also link your Medicare discount to a contactless bank card through Cal-ITP Benefits for faster verification on each trip [mst-fares-benefits].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li><li>MST: Benefits (Cal-ITP reduced fares)</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "Courtesy Cards are available to all disabled individuals free of charge with a physician’s written verification or a Medicare Card. Use the card for discounted fares and passes as proof of eligibility. Obtain an application in person at the MST Bus Stop Shop, 201 Pearl Street in Monterey, Marina Transit Exchange, 280 Reservation Road in Marina, Salinas Transit Center, 110 Salinas Street in Salinas, or here ( English application / Spanish application ).", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "MST now accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets. At this time, accepted mobile wallets include: Apple Pay , Google Pay, Samsung Pay , and Fitbit Pay . For more information, visit our Contactless Fare Payment Fast Facts and FAQs .\nCash\nCash is inserted into the farebox. Exact fare is not required. If you do not have exact fare the farebox will issue credit for use on future MST trips."], "expected_facts": ["Medicare"], "pair_id": "pair-edge-007", "is_reference": true, "language": "en"}
{"id": "edge-008", "question": "What proof of service does MST accept for the veteran discount?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nMST accepts the following proof of service for the veteran discount [doc:mst-fares]:\n\n- DD Form 214\n- DD Form 256N\n- US Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\n- Veterans of Foreign Wars ID card\n- Membership card from the American Legion\n- Disabled American Veterans ID card\n- CA driver license or ID card with \"veteran\" designation\n\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card using any of these documents, which can then be used as proof of eligibility for discounted fares [doc:mst-fares].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What proof of service does MST accept for the veteran discount?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>MST accepts the following proof of service for the veteran discount [mst-fares]:</p><p>- DD Form 214</p><p>- DD Form 256N</p><p>- US Department of Veterans Affairs ID card</p><p>- Veterans of Foreign Wars ID card</p><p>- Membership card from the American Legion</p><p>- Disabled American Veterans ID card</p><p>- CA driver license or ID card with \"veteran\" designation</p><p>Veterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card using any of these documents, which can then be used as proof of eligibility for discounted fares [mst-fares].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST) is partnering with veterans’ services organizations to select individuals needing transportation services. Selected veterans are provided free MST passes to honor their service to our country. For information email [email protected]\nVeterans Group Travel Training\nMonterey-Salinas Transit (MST) provides free fixed-route travel training to teach interested individuals how to safely and independently ride the MST bus system.\nTravel Training", "MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "To end Veteran homelessness in Monterey County. Each Veteran is paired with a dedicated VTC Case Manager who designs and supervises their recovery program while addressing medical, psychological, occupational, financial, educational, and social issues. They offer several housing programs, including emergency, transitional, and permanent supportive housing.\nhttps://www.vtcofcalifornia.org/programsandservices\nVocational Rehabilitation Specialist Inc\nTo assist veterans in need of housing, job placement, job skill training, and more.\nhttps://vrspecialists.com/", "MST now accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets. At this time, accepted mobile wallets include: Apple Pay , Google Pay, Samsung Pay , and Fitbit Pay . For more information, visit our Contactless Fare Payment Fast Facts and FAQs .\nCash\nCash is inserted into the farebox. Exact fare is not required. If you do not have exact fare the farebox will issue credit for use on future MST trips.", "The Office of Military & Veterans Affairs provides these services and helps with the following benefits:\nComprehensive benefits counseling\nClaims preparation and submission\nClaims follow-up to ensure final decisions are fully adjudicated\nInitiate and develop appeals when appropriate\nNetworking and advocacy with federal, state, and local agencies\nhttps://www.countyofmonterey.gov/government/departments-i-z/military-veterans#mva"], "expected_facts": ["DD Form 214"], "pair_id": "pair-edge-008", "is_reference": true, "language": "en"}
{"id": "edge-009", "question": "Does MST's veteran discount cover my wife too?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nMST's published policy states that a discount fare is available for \"Veterans (see also: Benefits), Veteran's spouse/caregiver\" [doc:mst-fares]. However, the published policy does not specify what documentation or proof is required to establish that relationship, or how a spouse or caregiver applies for or receives the discount.\n\nTo learn whether your wife qualifies and how to apply, please contact MST directly at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or visit mst.org.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Does MST's veteran discount cover my wife too?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>MST's published policy states that a discount fare is available for \"Veterans (see also: Benefits), Veteran's spouse/caregiver\" [mst-fares]. However, the published policy does not specify what documentation or proof is required to establish that relationship, or how a spouse or caregiver applies for or receives the discount.</p><p>To learn whether your wife qualifies and how to apply, please contact MST directly at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or visit mst.org.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "To end Veteran homelessness in Monterey County. Each Veteran is paired with a dedicated VTC Case Manager who designs and supervises their recovery program while addressing medical, psychological, occupational, financial, educational, and social issues. They offer several housing programs, including emergency, transitional, and permanent supportive housing.\nhttps://www.vtcofcalifornia.org/programsandservices\nVocational Rehabilitation Specialist Inc\nTo assist veterans in need of housing, job placement, job skill training, and more.\nhttps://vrspecialists.com/", "MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST) is partnering with veterans’ services organizations to select individuals needing transportation services. Selected veterans are provided free MST passes to honor their service to our country. For information email [email protected]\nVeterans Group Travel Training\nMonterey-Salinas Transit (MST) provides free fixed-route travel training to teach interested individuals how to safely and independently ride the MST bus system.\nTravel Training", "Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST) offers a taxi voucher program to assist veterans in accessing important locations within their communities.", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month."], "expected_facts": ["spouse"], "pair_id": "pair-edge-009", "is_reference": true, "language": "en"}
{"id": "edge-010", "question": "My son is 17. What does he pay to ride Yolobus?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Your son rides free on Yolobus. Youth ages 0–18 ride free [doc:yolobus-fares].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12. Please confirm with Yolobus for any updates.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>My son is 17. What does he pay to ride Yolobus?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Your son rides free on Yolobus. Youth ages 0–18 ride free [yolobus-fares].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12. Please confirm with Yolobus for any updates.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Yolobus: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes", "A Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). A Daily Pass can be purchased individually on board a Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect.", "A Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). A Daily Pass can be purchased individually onboard a Yolobus or by using Connect Card or the Transit Connect app.", "Yolobus-only and Sacramento Regional Transit Monthly Passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown, and through the first business day of the following month. Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or on the Transit Connect app, but not onboard a Yolobus.", "Yolobus-only and Sacramento Regional Transit Monthly Passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown, and through the first business day of the following month. Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or the Transit Connect mobile app, but not onboard a Yolobus.", "Yolobus fixed route bus fares vary depending on which type of service you ride. All below fares are effective July 1, 2025 – June 30, 2026 ."], "expected_facts": ["free"], "pair_id": "pair-edge-010", "is_reference": true, "language": "en"}
{"id": "edge-011", "question": "Is my 17-year-old free on MST like on Yolobus?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nNo. On **Yolobus**, youth ages 0–18 ride free [doc:yolobus-fares]. On **MST**, riders 18 years and under receive a **discount fare**, not a free fare [doc:mst-fares]. The published policy does not specify the discounted fare amount for that age group on MST.\n\nTo confirm the exact discounted fare for your 17-year-old on MST, contact MST directly at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or visit mst.org/fares/.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares", "yolobus-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Is my 17-year-old free on MST like on Yolobus?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>No. On **Yolobus**, youth ages 0–18 ride free [yolobus-fares]. On **MST**, riders 18 years and under receive a **discount fare**, not a free fare [mst-fares]. The published policy does not specify the discounted fare amount for that age group on MST.</p><p>To confirm the exact discounted fare for your 17-year-old on MST, contact MST directly at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or visit mst.org/fares/.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li><li>Yolobus: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "The GoCard is a stored value card. Passengers can add value to their card at any time by visiting an MST customer service location and use it like a debit card when riding the bus. The GoCard allows for speedy boarding. Simply tap your card on the farebox below the digital screen and the appropriate fare will be deducted. Plus passengers receive an additional 10% value each time the card is reloaded! For example, load your card with $20 and receive $22 value on your GoCard. Use the card when you need it, save the value when you don’t. $10 minimum purchase required.", "Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST) is partnering with veterans’ services organizations to select individuals needing transportation services. Selected veterans are provided free MST passes to honor their service to our country. For information email [email protected]\nVeterans Group Travel Training\nMonterey-Salinas Transit (MST) provides free fixed-route travel training to teach interested individuals how to safely and independently ride the MST bus system.\nTravel Training", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "The Transit Connect mobile fare app allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone. Accepted on Yolobus and SacRT, with Unitrans joining in summer 2026. Download the free app from the App Store or Google Play.", "Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes"], "expected_facts": ["18"]}
{"id": "edge-012", "question": "Do small children ride free on Santa Barbara MTD?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nYes. Children under 45 inches tall ride free on Santa Barbara MTD, with a limit of three children per adult [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Do small children ride free on Santa Barbara MTD?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>Yes. Children under 45 inches tall ride free on Santa Barbara MTD, with a limit of three children per adult [sbmtd-fares-passes].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["The table below shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD’s goal with this fare adjustment is to restore a portion of the approximately $3.0 million in annual FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities operating assistance that was lost when the 2020 Census found that the population of the Santa Barbara urbanized area exceeds 200,000 persons. The fare adjustment is estimated to generate approximately $1.5 million in increased revenue annually.", "New fares are now effective as of August 18, 2025. For more information click here .\nUCSB CAMPUS STORE\n1 UCEN Road Santa Barbara, CA 93106 805.893.3819\nEASTSIDE LIBRARY\n1102 East Montecito Street Santa Barbara, CA 93103 Vending Machine\nMTD TRANSIT CENTER\n1020 Chapala Street Santa Barbara, CA 93101 805. 963.3366\nMTD BUSINESS OFFICE\n550 Olive Street Santa Barbara, CA 93101 805. 963.3364 MTD accepts cash, a local imprinted check, or a money order (payable to “MTD”) as payment for passes.\nALBERTSON’S CARPINTERIA\n1018 Casitas Pass Road Carpinteria, CA 93013 Vending Machine\nGOLETA VALLEY COMMUNITY CENTER\n5679 Hollister Avenue Goleta, CA 93117 Vending Machine -Credit/Debit Card Only\nBY MAIL\nYou can order your passes through the mail by filling out & sending in an order form.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap.", "La tabla a continuación muestra la actualización de la estructura tarifaria con respecto a la actual. El objetivo de MTD con este ajuste tarifario es recuperar una parte de los aproximadamente $3.0 millones anuales en asistencia operativa del programa FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities, que se perdieron cuando el Censo de 2020 determinó que la población del área urbanizada de Santa Bárbara supera las 200,000 personas. Se estima que el ajuste tarifario generará aproximadamente $1.5 millones en ingresos adicionales anuales.", "All fares are one-way and may be paid with coins, bills, passes, or via Tap2Ride with a contactless bank card or mobile wallet. Exact change required when paying with cash; fareboxes do not give change and drivers cannot make change.\nYou can take advantage of pay-as-you-go discounted daily and monthly pass rates when you Tap2Ride.\nClick here to learn more, including how to get a contactless bank card or to add a bank card to the mobile wallet on your smart device.\nStandard\n$2.50 Regular one-way Youth (K-12th grade)\n$1.25 Seniors (age 65+) Persons with Disabilities* Persons with Medicare Cards* (Cash or Tap2Ride **) *Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\nFREE Children under 45 inches tall (three children per adult) Transfers (to complete a one-way trip) For more information on Transfers, click here .\n$6.00 Day Pass (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$2.50 Token for one-way fare", "Santa Barbara Metropolitan Transit District (MTD) is committed to providing reliable and affordable transit services to our community. While MTD’s fares have not changed in 16 years, cost pressures have come to the point of requiring a change in fare structure. We have carefully reviewed our fare system, received community feedback, and conducted a fare equity analysis. After careful consideration we adjusted our base fare from the previous fare of $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nImplementation of this new fare structure is a significant step toward assuring MTD’s services are dependable and economically sustainable in the long term."], "expected_facts": ["45 inches"], "pair_id": "pair-edge-012", "is_reference": true, "language": "en"}
{"id": "edge-013", "question": "Can I get the senior price on a Yolobus monthly pass, and does it work on express routes?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nYes, Yolobus offers senior monthly passes. The senior/disabled monthly pass price is $42.00 for Local Routes, Intercity Routes, or Express Routes [doc:yolobus-fares]. \n\nThe senior/disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity, and express routes — it does not require a separate upgrade to work on express [doc:yolobus-fares].\n\nTo qualify for the senior price, the published criteria are: you must be 62 or older, or disabled, and must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver's License or government-issued ID Card; or any other discount ID issued by another transit agency [doc:yolobus-fares].\n\nMonthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or the Transit Connect mobile app, but not onboard a Yolobus [doc:yolobus-purchasing].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares", "yolobus-purchasing"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Can I get the senior price on a Yolobus monthly pass, and does it work on express routes?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>Yes, Yolobus offers senior monthly passes. The senior/disabled monthly pass price is $42.00 for Local Routes, Intercity Routes, or Express Routes [yolobus-fares].</p><p>The senior/disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity, and express routes — it does not require a separate upgrade to work on express [yolobus-fares].</p><p>To qualify for the senior price, the published criteria are: you must be 62 or older, or disabled, and must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver's License or government-issued ID Card; or any other discount ID issued by another transit agency [yolobus-fares].</p><p>Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or the Transit Connect mobile app, but not onboard a Yolobus [yolobus-purchasing].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Yolobus: Fares</li><li>Yolobus: Purchasing a Ticket or Pass</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes", "ADA-eligible riders can use ADA paratransit (“Yolobus Special”) service. The Yolobus Special fares vary by distance (local vs. intercity).\nADA Paratransit (\"Yolobus Special\") Products\nADA Paratransit Local Fare | $4.00\nADA Paratransit Intercity Fare | $4.50\nADA Paratransit Premium Fare* | $6.00\n* Premium service is available to medical facilities only in portions of Sacramento and Vacaville that are beyond ¾ miles of Yolobus fixed routes. Destinations within ¾ mile of a Yolobus fixed route are charged the Local or Intercity fare, whichever is applicable\nHow to purchase a ticket or a pass\nMore information on how to purchase a ticket or a pass is available here", "Yolobus-only and Sacramento Regional Transit Monthly Passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown, and through the first business day of the following month. Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or on the Transit Connect app, but not onboard a Yolobus.", "Yolobus-only and Sacramento Regional Transit Monthly Passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown, and through the first business day of the following month. Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or the Transit Connect mobile app, but not onboard a Yolobus.", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required, and bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center.\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services.\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Thursday, excluding holidays 9am – 12pm; 1pm – 4pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets, and/or Passes onto Connect Cards", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required as our bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center .\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services:\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Thursday, excluding holidays 9am – 12pm; 1pm – 4pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets and/or Passes onto Connect Cards\nAlternative ticket and pass sales location in Sacramento\nTickets, passes and Connect Card services are also available in Sacramento at the Sacramento Regional Transit District (SacRT) Customer Service and Sales Center, 1225 R St., Sacramento (916) 321-2877\nSingle Ride\nTickets are for a one-way trip. A ticket can be purchased individually on Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect."], "expected_facts": ["$42"]}
{"id": "edge-014", "question": "Do UCSB students ride SBMTD buses for free?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nYes. UCSB students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding, and they ride MTD buses for no additional charge. [doc:sbmtd-farechange] This is part of an agreement between MTD and the University of California, Santa Barbara, whereby a portion of students' registration fees are provided to MTD in exchange for this benefit. [doc:sbmtd-farechange]\n\nThe student ID is valid from the first day of the quarter until 2 weeks into the next quarter. [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes] Note that UCSB & SBCC cards are not valid on the Downtown-Waterfront Shuttle. [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-farechange", "sbmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Do UCSB students ride SBMTD buses for free?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>Yes. UCSB students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding, and they ride MTD buses for no additional charge. [sbmtd-farechange] This is part of an agreement between MTD and the University of California, Santa Barbara, whereby a portion of students' registration fees are provided to MTD in exchange for this benefit. [sbmtd-farechange]</p><p>The student ID is valid from the first day of the quarter until 2 weeks into the next quarter. [sbmtd-fares-passes] Note that UCSB &amp; SBCC cards are not valid on the Downtown-Waterfront Shuttle. [sbmtd-fares-passes]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fare Change</li><li>SBMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Passengers with disabilities may be eligible for the Mobility Pass, a reduced fare to ride MTD buses.\nTo obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card, passengers must have an application completed by a qualified physician or an MTD-certified social service agency. You may download an application here , or get one at the MTD Transit Center, or call 805.963.3366 for more information.\nPassengers may also present a disabled photo ID card from another transit agency, a valid Medicare ID card, or a valid California DMV Disabled Placard Receipt to obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card.\nIf the photo ID card is lost, a replacement fee of $5.00 will be charged.\nUCSB & SBCC STUDENTS RIDE FREE\nUCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.", "UCSB and SBCC Student Bus Pass Programs : All UCSB & SBCC students pay a transportation fee and may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.\nDiscounted Fare Programs: Individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth are all eligible for reduced fares. Discounts are also available for pre-paid passes including Day passes, 10 – Ride passes, and 30 – Day passes.\nFare Capping: Pay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet. You can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nFree Transfers: If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus. Transfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. Free transfers work as follows: upon request, riders can receive a free 60 minute transfer to be used to board a second bus, heading in the same direction (i.e., an outbound Line 20 to an outbound Line 6). Riders may also receive a transfer on that second bus that is good for 30 minutes, also headed in the same direction. You can find more information on free transfers here .\nOther Transfers: With a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!\nPacific Surfliner’s Transit Transfer Program: On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.\nAlternative Funding Sources: MTD is actively seeking new funding sources. MTD continues to apply to grant programs to operate and improve service, maintain and replace vehicles, and enhance transit facilities throughout the district.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap.", "UCSB – First day of the quarter until 2 weeks into the next quarter. SBCC – 7 calendar days before the first day of classes until the last day of final exams, except Fall semester stickers are valid until 7 calendar days before Spring classes begin.\nUCSB & SBCC cards are not valid on the Downtown-Waterfront Shuttle.\n2025-2026 Valid Dates for Student Registration Stickers as Prepaid Bus Fare\nSBCC\nFall | Aug 18 – Jan 19\nSpring | Jan 19 – May 23\nSummer | May 25 – Aug 8\nUCSB\nFall | Sep 21 – Jan 15\nWinter | Jan 5 – Apr 10\nSpring | Mar 30 – July 3\nSummer | Jun 22 – Oct 2", "On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus or shuttle and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.", "If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus.\nTransfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. The transfer is valid for up to 60 minutes on the second bus. In the rare case that you need to transfer to a third bus to reach your destination, ask the driver on your second bus to give you another transfer. This transfer will be valid for 30 minutes.\nTransfers are valid to complete your one-way trip. Transfers are not valid for return trips. Passengers who use MTD’s 30 day passes will not be issued transfers. Use your pass on each bus in any direction.\nIt is MTD policy that drivers may wait up to 3 minutes beyond their scheduled departure times for transferring passengers. If you are concerned about catching your connecting bus, request that your driver call ahead to ask your next driver to wait for you.\nDuring peak-hour times when there is more frequent bus service, Lines 1, 2, 6 & 11 will not wait. There will be another bus in 10 minutes.\nMTD transfers are not valid on the Downtown and Waterfront Shuttles.\nOther Transfers\nWith a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!"], "expected_facts": ["student ID"]}
{"id": "edge-015", "question": "Is the bus free for Hartnell College students?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes, based on policies published as of 2026-06-17:\n\nHartnell students ride FREE on all routes with a valid photo student ID during the Fall and Spring semesters. [doc:mst-fares]\n\nThis applies to Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST). If you have questions about how to obtain or use your student ID for this benefit, contact MST directly.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Is the bus free for Hartnell College students?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes, based on policies published as of 2026-06-17:</p><p>Hartnell students ride FREE on all routes with a valid photo student ID during the Fall and Spring semesters. [mst-fares]</p><p>This applies to Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST). If you have questions about how to obtain or use your student ID for this benefit, contact MST directly.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Hartnell students ride FREE on all routes with a valid photo student ID during the Fall and Spring semesters.\nMPC students ride FREE on all routes with a valid photo student ID during the Fall and Spring semesters.\nCSUMB students ride FREE on all routes with a valid photo student ID during the Fall and Spring semesters.", "SacRT offers fare-free transit for students in Transitional Kindergarten through 12th grade with the RydeFreeRT program and has a partnership with Los Rios and Sacramento State for college students to ride SacRT.\nCalifornia State Workers\nSEIU members have access to free transit through SacRT! Other state employee unions are eligible for transit subsidies to cover most of your commute costs.", "Single Ride Ticket\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$10.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$20.00\n*Discount (senior, disabled or student) monthly or semi-monthly stickers must be affixed to a valid SacRT photo ID, with the exception of the student stickers, which may be attached to a school issued photo ID.\n**Many students in grades TK through 12, are eligible for free transit passes. Check with your school district for details or visit RydeFreeRT.\nBus\nPassengers are required to pay a basic or discount single fare for each trip or may purchase a daily pass valid for unlimited rides on that day.\nLight Rail\nSingle ride tickets are valid for 90 minutes from time of validation on light rail only, or pay the 25 cent transfer fee when boarding a bus.\nFare Accepted by Other Transit Agencies\nYour SacRT ticket or pass maybe eligible to transfer to and from other transit agencies. For more detailed fare info or a list of SacRT fares accepted by other transit agencies, click here .", "Cal Poly Humboldt students , faculty, and staff can ride A&MRTS, RTS, ETS, SH, NSE 299, and NSE 101 using their ID cards (Flex microtransit, and Dial-A-Ride routes are not included)!\nStudents : Just show and swipe your current student ID when you board. All of your rides are free!\nStaff & Faculty : you can buy into the Jack Pass ID program for $60 per semester. Get more info from HSU Parking & Commuter Services .\nQuestions?\nIf you have any questions call Humboldt Transit Authority at 707-443-0826.", "Passengers with disabilities may be eligible for the Mobility Pass, a reduced fare to ride MTD buses.\nTo obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card, passengers must have an application completed by a qualified physician or an MTD-certified social service agency. You may download an application here , or get one at the MTD Transit Center, or call 805.963.3366 for more information.\nPassengers may also present a disabled photo ID card from another transit agency, a valid Medicare ID card, or a valid California DMV Disabled Placard Receipt to obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card.\nIf the photo ID card is lost, a replacement fee of $5.00 will be charged.\nUCSB & SBCC STUDENTS RIDE FREE\nUCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.", "UCSB and SBCC Student Bus Pass Programs : All UCSB & SBCC students pay a transportation fee and may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.\nDiscounted Fare Programs: Individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth are all eligible for reduced fares. Discounts are also available for pre-paid passes including Day passes, 10 – Ride passes, and 30 – Day passes.\nFare Capping: Pay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet. You can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nFree Transfers: If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus. Transfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. Free transfers work as follows: upon request, riders can receive a free 60 minute transfer to be used to board a second bus, heading in the same direction (i.e., an outbound Line 20 to an outbound Line 6). Riders may also receive a transfer on that second bus that is good for 30 minutes, also headed in the same direction. You can find more information on free transfers here .\nOther Transfers: With a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!\nPacific Surfliner’s Transit Transfer Program: On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.\nAlternative Funding Sources: MTD is actively seeking new funding sources. MTD continues to apply to grant programs to operate and improve service, maintain and replace vehicles, and enhance transit facilities throughout the district."], "expected_facts": ["free"]}
{"id": "edge-016", "question": "I have a disabled rider ID from another transit agency. Will MST honor it?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nMST honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems [doc:mst-fares]. However, the published policy does not specify whether that includes disabled rider IDs from other agencies, or what documentation MST requires to verify eligibility when you board.\n\nTo confirm whether your specific disabled rider ID will be accepted, contact MST directly:\n- Phone: 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871)\n- Email or visit an MST customer service location (Bus Stop Shop at 201 Pearl Street in Monterey, Marina Transit Exchange at 280 Reservation Road in Marina, or Salinas Transit Center at 110 Salinas Street in Salinas)\n\nAlternatively, you can apply for an MST Courtesy Card free of charge with a physician's written verification or a Medicare Card [doc:mst-fares].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I have a disabled rider ID from another transit agency. Will MST honor it?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>MST honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems [mst-fares]. However, the published policy does not specify whether that includes disabled rider IDs from other agencies, or what documentation MST requires to verify eligibility when you board.</p><p>To confirm whether your specific disabled rider ID will be accepted, contact MST directly:</p><p>- Phone: 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871)</p><p>- Email or visit an MST customer service location (Bus Stop Shop at 201 Pearl Street in Monterey, Marina Transit Exchange at 280 Reservation Road in Marina, or Salinas Transit Center at 110 Salinas Street in Salinas)</p><p>Alternatively, you can apply for an MST Courtesy Card free of charge with a physician's written verification or a Medicare Card [mst-fares].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST) is partnering with veterans’ services organizations to select individuals needing transportation services. Selected veterans are provided free MST passes to honor their service to our country. For information email [email protected]\nVeterans Group Travel Training\nMonterey-Salinas Transit (MST) provides free fixed-route travel training to teach interested individuals how to safely and independently ride the MST bus system.\nTravel Training", "MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "MST now accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets. At this time, accepted mobile wallets include: Apple Pay , Google Pay, Samsung Pay , and Fitbit Pay . For more information, visit our Contactless Fare Payment Fast Facts and FAQs .\nCash\nCash is inserted into the farebox. Exact fare is not required. If you do not have exact fare the farebox will issue credit for use on future MST trips.", "Courtesy Cards are available to all disabled individuals free of charge with a physician’s written verification or a Medicare Card. Use the card for discounted fares and passes as proof of eligibility. Obtain an application in person at the MST Bus Stop Shop, 201 Pearl Street in Monterey, Marina Transit Exchange, 280 Reservation Road in Marina, Salinas Transit Center, 110 Salinas Street in Salinas, or here ( English application / Spanish application ).", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month."]}
{"id": "edge-017", "question": "How do I get a reduced-fare photo ID for Yolobus and what does it require?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\n**How to obtain a reduced-fare photo ID:**\n\nYou can obtain a reduced fare photo ID by visiting either of these locations:\n\n1. **Yolo Transportation District**  \n   350 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776  \n   Mon–Thu, 9:00 AM–Noon, 1:00–4:00 PM  \n   (530) 661-0816\n\n2. **SacRT Customer Service & Sales Center**  \n   1225 R Street, Sacramento, CA 95811  \n   Mon–Fri, 8:30 AM–5:00 PM  \n   (916) 321-2877\n\n[doc:yolobus-reduced-fare-id]\n\n**Who qualifies:**\n\nThe published policy does not specify the eligibility criteria or documentation requirements for obtaining the ID itself. The policy states that seniors (62+) and disabled riders can take advantage of reduced fares and must show proper identification when purchasing fares and boarding, [doc:yolobus-reduced-fare-id] but the specific process and proof needed to *obtain* the photo ID are not detailed in the passages provided.\n\nFor details on what you need to bring or how to apply, contact one of the offices above.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-reduced-fare-id"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How do I get a reduced-fare photo ID for Yolobus and what does it require?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>**How to obtain a reduced-fare photo ID:**</p><p>You can obtain a reduced fare photo ID by visiting either of these locations:</p><p>1. **Yolo Transportation District**</p><p>350 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776</p><p>Mon–Thu, 9:00 AM–Noon, 1:00–4:00 PM</p><p>(530) 661-0816</p><p>2. **SacRT Customer Service &amp; Sales Center**</p><p>1225 R Street, Sacramento, CA 95811</p><p>Mon–Fri, 8:30 AM–5:00 PM</p><p>(916) 321-2877</p><p>[yolobus-reduced-fare-id]</p><p>**Who qualifies:**</p><p>The published policy does not specify the eligibility criteria or documentation requirements for obtaining the ID itself. The policy states that seniors (62+) and disabled riders can take advantage of reduced fares and must show proper identification when purchasing fares and boarding, [yolobus-reduced-fare-id] but the specific process and proof needed to *obtain* the photo ID are not detailed in the passages provided.</p><p>For details on what you need to bring or how to apply, contact one of the offices above.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Yolobus: Reduced Fare ID</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Our senior (62+) and disabled riders can take advantage of Yolobus’ reduced fares. To qualify for reduced fares, riders must show proper identification when purchasing fares and to bus operators when boarding.\nQualifying riders may obtain a reduced fare photo ID by visiting the Yolo Transportation District office or SacRT Customer Service & Sales Center:\nYolo Transportation District\n350 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776 (Mon-Thu, 9:00 AM-Noon, 1:00-4:00 PM)\n(530) 661-0816", "Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes", "1225 R Street, Sacramento, CA 95811 (Mon-Fri, 8:30 AM-5:00 PM)\n(916) 321-2877\nOther forms of IDs that qualify for a discount fare includes:\nMedicare Card\nCalifornia Driver’s License\nCalifornia ID Card\nOther transits agencies’ Disabled ID (such as Unitrans, SacRT, etc.)", "ADA-eligible riders can use ADA paratransit (“Yolobus Special”) service. The Yolobus Special fares vary by distance (local vs. intercity).\nADA Paratransit (\"Yolobus Special\") Products\nADA Paratransit Local Fare | $4.00\nADA Paratransit Intercity Fare | $4.50\nADA Paratransit Premium Fare* | $6.00\n* Premium service is available to medical facilities only in portions of Sacramento and Vacaville that are beyond ¾ miles of Yolobus fixed routes. Destinations within ¾ mile of a Yolobus fixed route are charged the Local or Intercity fare, whichever is applicable\nHow to purchase a ticket or a pass\nMore information on how to purchase a ticket or a pass is available here", "Riders have many choices when paying their fares. Fares may be paid on board the bus with cash, but contactless payment is more efficient and reduces bus boarding times—getting riders to their destinations faster. Contactless fare payment options include Connect Card and the Transit Connect mobile app.", "Riders have many choices when paying their fares. While fares can be paid onboard the bus with cash, contactless payment is more efficient and reduces bus boarding times—getting riders to their destinations faster. Contactless fare payment options include the Connect Card and the Transit Connect mobile app."], "expected_facts": ["Woodland"]}
{"id": "edge-018", "question": "What happens if I lose my SBMTD reduced fare photo ID card?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "If you lose your SBMTD reduced fare photo ID card, a replacement fee of $5.00 will be charged. [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12, I recommend contacting MTD at 805.963.3366 for details on how to request a replacement.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What happens if I lose my SBMTD reduced fare photo ID card?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>If you lose your SBMTD reduced fare photo ID card, a replacement fee of $5.00 will be charged. [sbmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12, I recommend contacting MTD at 805.963.3366 for details on how to request a replacement.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Passengers with disabilities may be eligible for the Mobility Pass, a reduced fare to ride MTD buses.\nTo obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card, passengers must have an application completed by a qualified physician or an MTD-certified social service agency. You may download an application here , or get one at the MTD Transit Center, or call 805.963.3366 for more information.\nPassengers may also present a disabled photo ID card from another transit agency, a valid Medicare ID card, or a valid California DMV Disabled Placard Receipt to obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card.\nIf the photo ID card is lost, a replacement fee of $5.00 will be charged.\nUCSB & SBCC STUDENTS RIDE FREE\nUCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.", "All fares are one-way and may be paid with coins, bills, passes, or via Tap2Ride with a contactless bank card or mobile wallet. Exact change required when paying with cash; fareboxes do not give change and drivers cannot make change.\nYou can take advantage of pay-as-you-go discounted daily and monthly pass rates when you Tap2Ride.\nClick here to learn more, including how to get a contactless bank card or to add a bank card to the mobile wallet on your smart device.\nStandard\n$2.50 Regular one-way Youth (K-12th grade)\n$1.25 Seniors (age 65+) Persons with Disabilities* Persons with Medicare Cards* (Cash or Tap2Ride **) *Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\nFREE Children under 45 inches tall (three children per adult) Transfers (to complete a one-way trip) For more information on Transfers, click here .\n$6.00 Day Pass (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$2.50 Token for one-way fare", "Santa Barbara Metropolitan Transit District (MTD) is committed to providing reliable and affordable transit services to our community. While MTD’s fares have not changed in 16 years, cost pressures have come to the point of requiring a change in fare structure. We have carefully reviewed our fare system, received community feedback, and conducted a fare equity analysis. After careful consideration we adjusted our base fare from the previous fare of $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nImplementation of this new fare structure is a significant step toward assuring MTD’s services are dependable and economically sustainable in the long term.", "The table below shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD’s goal with this fare adjustment is to restore a portion of the approximately $3.0 million in annual FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities operating assistance that was lost when the 2020 Census found that the population of the Santa Barbara urbanized area exceeds 200,000 persons. The fare adjustment is estimated to generate approximately $1.5 million in increased revenue annually.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap.", "10-RIDE PASS Discounted\n$17.50 Adult\n$12.50 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$8.50 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* Apply here for persons with disabilities.\n*Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. MTD can accept cash, a local imprinted check, or a money order (payable to “MTD”) as payment for passes.\n30-DAY PASS Discounted\n$80.00 Adult (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$65.00 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$30.00 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* (purchased in advance or via Tap2Ride**) * Apply here for a physical pass for persons with disabilities. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\n1-DAY PASS\nSystemwide Day Pass $6.00 (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride) Good on all routes. Valid for unlimited trips in one calendar day.\n30-Day Passes are valid for 30 days from first use. 30-Day passes are not transferable and transfers are not available with this pass.\n10-Ride Passes do not expire.\nImproper use of passes may result in revocation or confiscation.\nAll pass sales are final. No refunds. Passes have no cash value."], "expected_facts": ["$5"]}
{"id": "edge-019", "question": "Does my 8-year-old have to pay to ride SacRT?", "provenance": {"source": "SacRT published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nSacRT offers fare-free transit for students in Transitional Kindergarten through 12th grade through the RydeFreeRT program [doc:sacrt-fares]. An 8-year-old in grades TK–12 may be eligible for this free pass.\n\nHowever, the published policy does not specify how eligibility is documented or verified. To confirm whether your child qualifies and how to enroll, check with your school district or visit RydeFreeRT [doc:sacrt-fares].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sacrt-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Does my 8-year-old have to pay to ride SacRT?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>SacRT offers fare-free transit for students in Transitional Kindergarten through 12th grade through the RydeFreeRT program [sacrt-fares]. An 8-year-old in grades TK–12 may be eligible for this free pass.</p><p>However, the published policy does not specify how eligibility is documented or verified. To confirm whether your child qualifies and how to enroll, check with your school district or visit RydeFreeRT [sacrt-fares].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SacRT: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Single Ride Ticket\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$10.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$20.00\n*Discount (senior, disabled or student) monthly or semi-monthly stickers must be affixed to a valid SacRT photo ID, with the exception of the student stickers, which may be attached to a school issued photo ID.\n**Many students in grades TK through 12, are eligible for free transit passes. Check with your school district for details or visit RydeFreeRT.\nBus\nPassengers are required to pay a basic or discount single fare for each trip or may purchase a daily pass valid for unlimited rides on that day.\nLight Rail\nSingle ride tickets are valid for 90 minutes from time of validation on light rail only, or pay the 25 cent transfer fee when boarding a bus.\nFare Accepted by Other Transit Agencies\nYour SacRT ticket or pass maybe eligible to transfer to and from other transit agencies. For more detailed fare info or a list of SacRT fares accepted by other transit agencies, click here .", "SacRT offers fare-free transit for students in Transitional Kindergarten through 12th grade with the RydeFreeRT program and has a partnership with Los Rios and Sacramento State for college students to ride SacRT.\nCalifornia State Workers\nSEIU members have access to free transit through SacRT! Other state employee unions are eligible for transit subsidies to cover most of your commute costs.", "Our mobile fare app ZipPass allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus or light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nNeed a ticket for your next SacRT Light Rail trip?\nOur fare vending machines located at all SacRT light rail stations are simple to use!\n1. Choose your ticket or pass\n2. Pick your quantity\n3. Complete your payment (cash or card)\n4. Take your ticket\nTap2Ride\nTap your contactless card, mobile device or mobile wallet on Tap2Ride devices on bus and SacRT GO vehicles to pay fare.", "Our mobile fare app Transit Connect allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus and light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nLearn more at sacrt.com/TransitConnect .", "SacRT offers a variety of fare options to meet your travel needs.\nImportant Notice for ZipPass Users\nLast day to use passes/tickets: April 30, 2026", "All fares are subject to change.\nExact change required only on bus; operators do not carry cash. Light rail station fare vending machines accept both cash/credit or debit. Pre-paid tickets and passes must be validated at the fare vending machines before boarding.\nMonthly passes cover the full month, and semi-monthly passes can be purchased for the 1st – 15th or 16th – end of the month.\nAll passengers must pay basic fare unless eligible for discount fare or free fare. View the fare categories page to see if you qualify for senior, disabled or student fare.\nAge 19-61 - Basic\nSingle Ride Ticket\n$2.50\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$7.00\nSemi-Monthly Pass\n$50.00\nMonthly Pass\n$100.00\nSenior (age 62+) - Discount\nSingle\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$25.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$50.00\nSuper Senior Monthly Pass/Sticker (age 75+)*\n$40.00\nPerson with Disabilities - Discount\nSingle\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$25.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$50.00"], "expected_facts": ["free"]}
{"id": "edge-020", "question": "My organization wants to buy 25 monthly GoPasses from MST. Is there a bulk discount?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes, MST offers a bulk discount for organizations. Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nFor 25 monthly GoPasses, your organization qualifies for the **35% Discount**, which brings the Monthly GoPass price to **$45.00** [doc:mst-fares].\n\nTo enroll in the Group Discount Program, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871). Once enrolled, you can place your order online [doc:mst-fares].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>My organization wants to buy 25 monthly GoPasses from MST. Is there a bulk discount?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes, MST offers a bulk discount for organizations. Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>For 25 monthly GoPasses, your organization qualifies for the **35% Discount**, which brings the Monthly GoPass price to **$45.00** [mst-fares].</p><p>To enroll in the Group Discount Program, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871). Once enrolled, you can place your order online [mst-fares].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST) is partnering with veterans’ services organizations to select individuals needing transportation services. Selected veterans are provided free MST passes to honor their service to our country. For information email [email protected]\nVeterans Group Travel Training\nMonterey-Salinas Transit (MST) provides free fixed-route travel training to teach interested individuals how to safely and independently ride the MST bus system.\nTravel Training", "MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "For your convenience, MST GoPasses are available at MST Customer Service locations and pass outlet locations throughout your community.\nCarmel Carmel Drug Store, San Carlos & Ocean\nGonzales City Hall, 147 Fourth St.\nGreenfield City Hall, 45 El Camino Real\nKing City City Hall, 212 S. Vanderhurst Ave.\nMarina Marina Transit Exchange, 280 Reservation Road\nMonterey MST Bus Stop Shop, 201 Pearl Street\nSalinas Costco, 1339 N. Davis Rd. (Regular GoPass only) Salinas Transit Center, 110 Salinas Street\nSand City Costco, 801 Tioga Ave. (Regular GoPass only)\nSeaside Mal’s Market, 1264 Noche Buena\nGopasses and Gocards may also be purchased anytime online at the MST Store , by phone at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or by mail by sending your check, credit card information or money order to: GoPasses, 19 Upper Ragsdale Drive, Suite 200, Monterey, CA 93940."], "expected_facts": ["35%"]}
{"id": "edge-021", "question": "Can I use an SBMTD transfer to come back home later in the day?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "No. According to SBMTD policy, \"Transfers are valid to complete your one-way trip. Transfers are not valid for return trips.\" [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]\n\nA transfer allows you to board up to two additional buses to complete a single outbound journey, but you cannot use it to return home later. For a return trip, you would need to pay a new fare or use a pass.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Can I use an SBMTD transfer to come back home later in the day?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>No. According to SBMTD policy, \"Transfers are valid to complete your one-way trip. Transfers are not valid for return trips.\" [sbmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>A transfer allows you to board up to two additional buses to complete a single outbound journey, but you cannot use it to return home later. For a return trip, you would need to pay a new fare or use a pass.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus.\nTransfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. The transfer is valid for up to 60 minutes on the second bus. In the rare case that you need to transfer to a third bus to reach your destination, ask the driver on your second bus to give you another transfer. This transfer will be valid for 30 minutes.\nTransfers are valid to complete your one-way trip. Transfers are not valid for return trips. Passengers who use MTD’s 30 day passes will not be issued transfers. Use your pass on each bus in any direction.\nIt is MTD policy that drivers may wait up to 3 minutes beyond their scheduled departure times for transferring passengers. If you are concerned about catching your connecting bus, request that your driver call ahead to ask your next driver to wait for you.\nDuring peak-hour times when there is more frequent bus service, Lines 1, 2, 6 & 11 will not wait. There will be another bus in 10 minutes.\nMTD transfers are not valid on the Downtown and Waterfront Shuttles.\nOther Transfers\nWith a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!", "UCSB and SBCC Student Bus Pass Programs : All UCSB & SBCC students pay a transportation fee and may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.\nDiscounted Fare Programs: Individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth are all eligible for reduced fares. Discounts are also available for pre-paid passes including Day passes, 10 – Ride passes, and 30 – Day passes.\nFare Capping: Pay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet. You can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nFree Transfers: If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus. Transfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. Free transfers work as follows: upon request, riders can receive a free 60 minute transfer to be used to board a second bus, heading in the same direction (i.e., an outbound Line 20 to an outbound Line 6). Riders may also receive a transfer on that second bus that is good for 30 minutes, also headed in the same direction. You can find more information on free transfers here .\nOther Transfers: With a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!\nPacific Surfliner’s Transit Transfer Program: On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.\nAlternative Funding Sources: MTD is actively seeking new funding sources. MTD continues to apply to grant programs to operate and improve service, maintain and replace vehicles, and enhance transit facilities throughout the district.", "10-RIDE PASS Discounted\n$17.50 Adult\n$12.50 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$8.50 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* Apply here for persons with disabilities.\n*Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. MTD can accept cash, a local imprinted check, or a money order (payable to “MTD”) as payment for passes.\n30-DAY PASS Discounted\n$80.00 Adult (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$65.00 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$30.00 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* (purchased in advance or via Tap2Ride**) * Apply here for a physical pass for persons with disabilities. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\n1-DAY PASS\nSystemwide Day Pass $6.00 (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride) Good on all routes. Valid for unlimited trips in one calendar day.\n30-Day Passes are valid for 30 days from first use. 30-Day passes are not transferable and transfers are not available with this pass.\n10-Ride Passes do not expire.\nImproper use of passes may result in revocation or confiscation.\nAll pass sales are final. No refunds. Passes have no cash value.", "UCSB – First day of the quarter until 2 weeks into the next quarter. SBCC – 7 calendar days before the first day of classes until the last day of final exams, except Fall semester stickers are valid until 7 calendar days before Spring classes begin.\nUCSB & SBCC cards are not valid on the Downtown-Waterfront Shuttle.\n2025-2026 Valid Dates for Student Registration Stickers as Prepaid Bus Fare\nSBCC\nFall | Aug 18 – Jan 19\nSpring | Jan 19 – May 23\nSummer | May 25 – Aug 8\nUCSB\nFall | Sep 21 – Jan 15\nWinter | Jan 5 – Apr 10\nSpring | Mar 30 – July 3\nSummer | Jun 22 – Oct 2", "On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus or shuttle and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap."], "expected_facts": ["one-way"]}
{"id": "edge-022", "question": "I need three buses to get across Santa Barbara — does one transfer cover all of them?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nNo, one transfer does not cover all three buses. Here's how MTD's transfer system works:\n\nWhen you pay your fare on the first bus, ask the driver for a transfer. That transfer is valid for up to 60 minutes on your second bus. If you need a third bus, ask the driver on your second bus for another transfer, which will be valid for 30 minutes on that third bus. [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]\n\nSo you will receive two separate transfers to complete your three-bus trip, but both are free and can be used as your fare on those additional buses. [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I need three buses to get across Santa Barbara — does one transfer cover all of them?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>No, one transfer does not cover all three buses. Here's how MTD's transfer system works:</p><p>When you pay your fare on the first bus, ask the driver for a transfer. That transfer is valid for up to 60 minutes on your second bus. If you need a third bus, ask the driver on your second bus for another transfer, which will be valid for 30 minutes on that third bus. [sbmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>So you will receive two separate transfers to complete your three-bus trip, but both are free and can be used as your fare on those additional buses. [sbmtd-fares-passes]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus.\nTransfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. The transfer is valid for up to 60 minutes on the second bus. In the rare case that you need to transfer to a third bus to reach your destination, ask the driver on your second bus to give you another transfer. This transfer will be valid for 30 minutes.\nTransfers are valid to complete your one-way trip. Transfers are not valid for return trips. Passengers who use MTD’s 30 day passes will not be issued transfers. Use your pass on each bus in any direction.\nIt is MTD policy that drivers may wait up to 3 minutes beyond their scheduled departure times for transferring passengers. If you are concerned about catching your connecting bus, request that your driver call ahead to ask your next driver to wait for you.\nDuring peak-hour times when there is more frequent bus service, Lines 1, 2, 6 & 11 will not wait. There will be another bus in 10 minutes.\nMTD transfers are not valid on the Downtown and Waterfront Shuttles.\nOther Transfers\nWith a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!", "All fares are one-way and may be paid with coins, bills, passes, or via Tap2Ride with a contactless bank card or mobile wallet. Exact change required when paying with cash; fareboxes do not give change and drivers cannot make change.\nYou can take advantage of pay-as-you-go discounted daily and monthly pass rates when you Tap2Ride.\nClick here to learn more, including how to get a contactless bank card or to add a bank card to the mobile wallet on your smart device.\nStandard\n$2.50 Regular one-way Youth (K-12th grade)\n$1.25 Seniors (age 65+) Persons with Disabilities* Persons with Medicare Cards* (Cash or Tap2Ride **) *Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\nFREE Children under 45 inches tall (three children per adult) Transfers (to complete a one-way trip) For more information on Transfers, click here .\n$6.00 Day Pass (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$2.50 Token for one-way fare", "UCSB and SBCC Student Bus Pass Programs : All UCSB & SBCC students pay a transportation fee and may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.\nDiscounted Fare Programs: Individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth are all eligible for reduced fares. Discounts are also available for pre-paid passes including Day passes, 10 – Ride passes, and 30 – Day passes.\nFare Capping: Pay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet. You can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nFree Transfers: If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus. Transfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. Free transfers work as follows: upon request, riders can receive a free 60 minute transfer to be used to board a second bus, heading in the same direction (i.e., an outbound Line 20 to an outbound Line 6). Riders may also receive a transfer on that second bus that is good for 30 minutes, also headed in the same direction. You can find more information on free transfers here .\nOther Transfers: With a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!\nPacific Surfliner’s Transit Transfer Program: On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.\nAlternative Funding Sources: MTD is actively seeking new funding sources. MTD continues to apply to grant programs to operate and improve service, maintain and replace vehicles, and enhance transit facilities throughout the district.", "New fares are now effective as of August 18, 2025. For more information click here .\nUCSB CAMPUS STORE\n1 UCEN Road Santa Barbara, CA 93106 805.893.3819\nEASTSIDE LIBRARY\n1102 East Montecito Street Santa Barbara, CA 93103 Vending Machine\nMTD TRANSIT CENTER\n1020 Chapala Street Santa Barbara, CA 93101 805. 963.3366\nMTD BUSINESS OFFICE\n550 Olive Street Santa Barbara, CA 93101 805. 963.3364 MTD accepts cash, a local imprinted check, or a money order (payable to “MTD”) as payment for passes.\nALBERTSON’S CARPINTERIA\n1018 Casitas Pass Road Carpinteria, CA 93013 Vending Machine\nGOLETA VALLEY COMMUNITY CENTER\n5679 Hollister Avenue Goleta, CA 93117 Vending Machine -Credit/Debit Card Only\nBY MAIL\nYou can order your passes through the mail by filling out & sending in an order form.", "Santa Barbara Metropolitan Transit District (MTD) is committed to providing reliable and affordable transit services to our community. While MTD’s fares have not changed in 16 years, cost pressures have come to the point of requiring a change in fare structure. We have carefully reviewed our fare system, received community feedback, and conducted a fare equity analysis. After careful consideration we adjusted our base fare from the previous fare of $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nImplementation of this new fare structure is a significant step toward assuring MTD’s services are dependable and economically sustainable in the long term.", "Passengers with disabilities may be eligible for the Mobility Pass, a reduced fare to ride MTD buses.\nTo obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card, passengers must have an application completed by a qualified physician or an MTD-certified social service agency. You may download an application here , or get one at the MTD Transit Center, or call 805.963.3366 for more information.\nPassengers may also present a disabled photo ID card from another transit agency, a valid Medicare ID card, or a valid California DMV Disabled Placard Receipt to obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card.\nIf the photo ID card is lost, a replacement fee of $5.00 will be charged.\nUCSB & SBCC STUDENTS RIDE FREE\nUCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding."], "expected_facts": ["30 minutes"]}
{"id": "edge-023", "question": "I have a VCTC transfer slip. What do I pay on SBMTD?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nWith a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD. [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I have a VCTC transfer slip. What do I pay on SBMTD?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>With a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD. [sbmtd-fares-passes]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus.\nTransfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. The transfer is valid for up to 60 minutes on the second bus. In the rare case that you need to transfer to a third bus to reach your destination, ask the driver on your second bus to give you another transfer. This transfer will be valid for 30 minutes.\nTransfers are valid to complete your one-way trip. Transfers are not valid for return trips. Passengers who use MTD’s 30 day passes will not be issued transfers. Use your pass on each bus in any direction.\nIt is MTD policy that drivers may wait up to 3 minutes beyond their scheduled departure times for transferring passengers. If you are concerned about catching your connecting bus, request that your driver call ahead to ask your next driver to wait for you.\nDuring peak-hour times when there is more frequent bus service, Lines 1, 2, 6 & 11 will not wait. There will be another bus in 10 minutes.\nMTD transfers are not valid on the Downtown and Waterfront Shuttles.\nOther Transfers\nWith a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!", "UCSB and SBCC Student Bus Pass Programs : All UCSB & SBCC students pay a transportation fee and may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.\nDiscounted Fare Programs: Individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth are all eligible for reduced fares. Discounts are also available for pre-paid passes including Day passes, 10 – Ride passes, and 30 – Day passes.\nFare Capping: Pay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet. You can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nFree Transfers: If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus. Transfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. Free transfers work as follows: upon request, riders can receive a free 60 minute transfer to be used to board a second bus, heading in the same direction (i.e., an outbound Line 20 to an outbound Line 6). Riders may also receive a transfer on that second bus that is good for 30 minutes, also headed in the same direction. You can find more information on free transfers here .\nOther Transfers: With a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!\nPacific Surfliner’s Transit Transfer Program: On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.\nAlternative Funding Sources: MTD is actively seeking new funding sources. MTD continues to apply to grant programs to operate and improve service, maintain and replace vehicles, and enhance transit facilities throughout the district.", "Santa Barbara Metropolitan Transit District (MTD) is committed to providing reliable and affordable transit services to our community. While MTD’s fares have not changed in 16 years, cost pressures have come to the point of requiring a change in fare structure. We have carefully reviewed our fare system, received community feedback, and conducted a fare equity analysis. After careful consideration we adjusted our base fare from the previous fare of $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nImplementation of this new fare structure is a significant step toward assuring MTD’s services are dependable and economically sustainable in the long term.", "All fares are one-way and may be paid with coins, bills, passes, or via Tap2Ride with a contactless bank card or mobile wallet. Exact change required when paying with cash; fareboxes do not give change and drivers cannot make change.\nYou can take advantage of pay-as-you-go discounted daily and monthly pass rates when you Tap2Ride.\nClick here to learn more, including how to get a contactless bank card or to add a bank card to the mobile wallet on your smart device.\nStandard\n$2.50 Regular one-way Youth (K-12th grade)\n$1.25 Seniors (age 65+) Persons with Disabilities* Persons with Medicare Cards* (Cash or Tap2Ride **) *Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\nFREE Children under 45 inches tall (three children per adult) Transfers (to complete a one-way trip) For more information on Transfers, click here .\n$6.00 Day Pass (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$2.50 Token for one-way fare", "10-RIDE PASS Discounted\n$17.50 Adult\n$12.50 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$8.50 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* Apply here for persons with disabilities.\n*Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. MTD can accept cash, a local imprinted check, or a money order (payable to “MTD”) as payment for passes.\n30-DAY PASS Discounted\n$80.00 Adult (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$65.00 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$30.00 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* (purchased in advance or via Tap2Ride**) * Apply here for a physical pass for persons with disabilities. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\n1-DAY PASS\nSystemwide Day Pass $6.00 (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride) Good on all routes. Valid for unlimited trips in one calendar day.\n30-Day Passes are valid for 30 days from first use. 30-Day passes are not transferable and transfers are not available with this pass.\n10-Ride Passes do not expire.\nImproper use of passes may result in revocation or confiscation.\nAll pass sales are final. No refunds. Passes have no cash value.", "The table below shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD’s goal with this fare adjustment is to restore a portion of the approximately $3.0 million in annual FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities operating assistance that was lost when the 2020 Census found that the population of the Santa Barbara urbanized area exceeds 200,000 persons. The fare adjustment is estimated to generate approximately $1.5 million in increased revenue annually."], "expected_facts": ["$1.00"]}
{"id": "edge-024", "question": "I'm taking the Pacific Surfliner to Santa Barbara — can I ride MTD free with my Amtrak ticket?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes. On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus, and you can ride MTD for free [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm taking the Pacific Surfliner to Santa Barbara — can I ride MTD free with my Amtrak ticket?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes. On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus, and you can ride MTD for free [sbmtd-fares-passes].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus or shuttle and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.", "UCSB and SBCC Student Bus Pass Programs : All UCSB & SBCC students pay a transportation fee and may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.\nDiscounted Fare Programs: Individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth are all eligible for reduced fares. Discounts are also available for pre-paid passes including Day passes, 10 – Ride passes, and 30 – Day passes.\nFare Capping: Pay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet. You can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nFree Transfers: If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus. Transfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. Free transfers work as follows: upon request, riders can receive a free 60 minute transfer to be used to board a second bus, heading in the same direction (i.e., an outbound Line 20 to an outbound Line 6). Riders may also receive a transfer on that second bus that is good for 30 minutes, also headed in the same direction. You can find more information on free transfers here .\nOther Transfers: With a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!\nPacific Surfliner’s Transit Transfer Program: On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.\nAlternative Funding Sources: MTD is actively seeking new funding sources. MTD continues to apply to grant programs to operate and improve service, maintain and replace vehicles, and enhance transit facilities throughout the district.", "Programas de pases de autobús para estudiantes de UCSB y SBCC: todos los estudiantes de UCSB y SBCC pagan una tarifa de transporte y pueden usar su identificación de estudiante válida como pase de autobús al tocarla en la máquina de tarifas al abordar.\nProgramas de tarifas con descuento: las personas con discapacidades, las personas mayores y los jóvenes son elegibles para tarifas reducidas. También hay descuentos disponibles para pases prepagos, incluidos pases diarios, pases de 10 viajes y pases de 30 días.\nLímite de tarifas : el pago por uso (límite de tarifas) permite a los pasajeros viajar gratis después de haber pagado el equivalente a un pase diario o mensual al usar el pago sin contacto Tap2Ride. El límite de tarifas garantiza que no se le cobrará más de una cantidad específica por día o período de 30 días, sin importar cuántas veces viaje, siempre que pague con la misma tarjeta sin contacto o billetera móvil. Puede encontrar información adicional sobre el límite de tarifas de MTD y Tap2Ride aquí .\nTraslados gratuitos: si necesita usar más de un autobús para llegar a su destino, pídale al conductor un transbordo cuando pague su tarifa en el primer autobús. Los transbordos son gratuitos y se pueden usar como tarifa en hasta dos autobuses más. Los transbordos gratuitos funcionan de la siguiente manera: a pedido, los pasajeros pueden recibir un transbordo gratuito de 60 minutos para usar para abordar un segundo autobús, que se dirija en la misma dirección (es decir, una línea 20 de salida a una línea 6 de salida). Los pasajeros también pueden recibir una transferencia en ese segundo autobús que es válida por 30 minutos, también en la misma dirección. Puede encontrar más información sobre transferencias gratuitas aquí .\nOtras transferencias: ¡Con un comprobante de transferencia válido de VCTC, paga solo $1.00 para viajar en MTD!\nPrograma de transferencia de tránsito de Pacific Surfliner: el día que viaje en el tren Pacific Surfliner, muestre su boleto impreso o electrónico válido de Amtrak Pacific Surfliner al conductor cuando suba al autobús de conexión y viaje en MTD gratis. Visite Pacific Surfliner para obtener más información.\nFuentes de financiamiento alternativas: MTD está buscando activamente nuevas fuentes de financiamiento. MTD continúa solicitando subvenciones para operar y mejorar el servicio, mantener y reemplazar vehículos y mejorar las instalaciones de tránsito en todo el distrito.", "New fares are now effective as of August 18, 2025. For more information click here .\nUCSB CAMPUS STORE\n1 UCEN Road Santa Barbara, CA 93106 805.893.3819\nEASTSIDE LIBRARY\n1102 East Montecito Street Santa Barbara, CA 93103 Vending Machine\nMTD TRANSIT CENTER\n1020 Chapala Street Santa Barbara, CA 93101 805. 963.3366\nMTD BUSINESS OFFICE\n550 Olive Street Santa Barbara, CA 93101 805. 963.3364 MTD accepts cash, a local imprinted check, or a money order (payable to “MTD”) as payment for passes.\nALBERTSON’S CARPINTERIA\n1018 Casitas Pass Road Carpinteria, CA 93013 Vending Machine\nGOLETA VALLEY COMMUNITY CENTER\n5679 Hollister Avenue Goleta, CA 93117 Vending Machine -Credit/Debit Card Only\nBY MAIL\nYou can order your passes through the mail by filling out & sending in an order form.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap.", "The table below shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD’s goal with this fare adjustment is to restore a portion of the approximately $3.0 million in annual FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities operating assistance that was lost when the 2020 Census found that the population of the Santa Barbara urbanized area exceeds 200,000 persons. The fare adjustment is estimated to generate approximately $1.5 million in increased revenue annually."], "expected_facts": ["free"]}
{"id": "edge-025", "question": "Can I ride Yolobus with my UC Davis Aggie Card?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes, you can ride Yolobus with your UC Davis Aggie Card. According to the published policy, a UC Davis Aggie Card is valid for unlimited rides on Yolobus, including Express routes, provided it is for Undergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only [doc:yolobus-fares].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12, I recommend confirming with Yolobus that your card meets the current validity requirements before boarding.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Can I ride Yolobus with my UC Davis Aggie Card?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes, you can ride Yolobus with your UC Davis Aggie Card. According to the published policy, a UC Davis Aggie Card is valid for unlimited rides on Yolobus, including Express routes, provided it is for Undergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only [yolobus-fares].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12, I recommend confirming with Yolobus that your card meets the current validity requirements before boarding.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Yolobus: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["The following passes are good for unlimited rides on Yolobus, including Express routes.\nUC Davis Aggie Card | UC Davis Extension International Program ID | Sacramento State Student ID | Los Rios Student Access Card | South Natomas TMA Pass | Sacramento County DHA Pass\nUndergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only | with valid expiration date | with valid commuter sleeve | with valid sticker | with valid expiration date | with valid sticker\nUC Davis Aggie Card: Undergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only\nUC Davis Extension International Program ID: with valid expiration date\nSacramento State Student ID: with valid commuter sleeve\nLos Rios Student Access Card: with valid sticker\nSouth Natomas TMA Pass: with valid expiration date\nSacramento County DHA Pass: with valid sticker", "The following passes are good for unlimited rides on Yolobus, including Express routes.\nAll fares are effective July 1, 2025 – June 30, 2026.\nUC Davis Aggie Card | UC Davis Zip Pass | UC Davis Extension International Program ID | Sacramento State Student ID | Los Rios Student Access Card | South Natomas TMA Pass | Sacramento County DHA Pass\nUndergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only | with valid student ID | with valid expiration date | with valid commuter sleeve | with valid sticker | with valid expiration date | with valid sticker\nUC Davis Aggie Card: Undergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only\nUC Davis Zip Pass: with valid student ID\nUC Davis Extension International Program ID: with valid expiration date\nSacramento State Student ID: with valid commuter sleeve\nLos Rios Student Access Card: with valid sticker\nSouth Natomas TMA Pass: with valid expiration date\nSacramento County DHA Pass: with valid sticker", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required as our bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center .\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services:\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Thursday, excluding holidays 9am – 12pm; 1pm – 4pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets and/or Passes onto Connect Cards\nAlternative ticket and pass sales location in Sacramento\nTickets, passes and Connect Card services are also available in Sacramento at the Sacramento Regional Transit District (SacRT) Customer Service and Sales Center, 1225 R St., Sacramento (916) 321-2877\nSingle Ride\nTickets are for a one-way trip. A ticket can be purchased individually on Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect.", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required, and bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center.\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services.\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Thursday, excluding holidays 9am – 12pm; 1pm – 4pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets, and/or Passes onto Connect Cards", "A Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). A Daily Pass can be purchased individually on board a Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect.", "A Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). A Daily Pass can be purchased individually onboard a Yolobus or by using Connect Card or the Transit Connect app."], "expected_facts": ["Undergrad"]}
{"id": "edge-026", "question": "What does Yolobus Special paratransit cost for a local trip versus an intercity one?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\n**Yolobus Special (ADA paratransit) fares:**\n\n- **Local trip:** $4.00 [doc:yolobus-fares]\n- **Intercity trip:** $4.50 [doc:yolobus-fares]\n\nThere is also a Premium Fare of $6.00, which applies to medical facilities only in portions of Sacramento and Vacaville that are beyond ¾ miles of Yolobus fixed routes. Destinations within ¾ mile of a Yolobus fixed route are charged the Local or Intercity fare instead. [doc:yolobus-fares]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What does Yolobus Special paratransit cost for a local trip versus an intercity one?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>**Yolobus Special (ADA paratransit) fares:**</p><p>- **Local trip:** $4.00 [yolobus-fares]</p><p>- **Intercity trip:** $4.50 [yolobus-fares]</p><p>There is also a Premium Fare of $6.00, which applies to medical facilities only in portions of Sacramento and Vacaville that are beyond ¾ miles of Yolobus fixed routes. Destinations within ¾ mile of a Yolobus fixed route are charged the Local or Intercity fare instead. [yolobus-fares]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Yolobus: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["ADA-eligible riders can use ADA paratransit (“Yolobus Special”) service. The Yolobus Special fares vary by distance (local vs. intercity).\nADA Paratransit (\"Yolobus Special\") Products\nADA Paratransit Local Fare | $4.00\nADA Paratransit Intercity Fare | $4.50\nADA Paratransit Premium Fare* | $6.00\n* Premium service is available to medical facilities only in portions of Sacramento and Vacaville that are beyond ¾ miles of Yolobus fixed routes. Destinations within ¾ mile of a Yolobus fixed route are charged the Local or Intercity fare, whichever is applicable\nHow to purchase a ticket or a pass\nMore information on how to purchase a ticket or a pass is available here", "Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes", "Tickets, passes, and Connect Card services are also available in Sacramento at the Sacramento Regional Transit District (SacRT) Customer Service and Sales Center, 1225 R St., Sacramento (916) 321-2877\nSingle Ride\nTickets are for a one-way trip. A ticket can be purchased individually on Yolobus or by using Connect Card or the Transit Connect app.", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required as our bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center .\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services:\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Thursday, excluding holidays 9am – 12pm; 1pm – 4pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets and/or Passes onto Connect Cards\nAlternative ticket and pass sales location in Sacramento\nTickets, passes and Connect Card services are also available in Sacramento at the Sacramento Regional Transit District (SacRT) Customer Service and Sales Center, 1225 R St., Sacramento (916) 321-2877\nSingle Ride\nTickets are for a one-way trip. A ticket can be purchased individually on Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect.", "If you are planning to connect with another Yolobus route to complete your trip, ask the driver for a transfer slip. You may also purchase a single fare + transfer in the Transit Connect app. Learn more about transfers >", "If you are planning to connect with another Yolobus route to complete your trip, ask the driver for a transfer slip. You may also purchase a single fare + transfer in the Transit Connect app. Learn more about transfers ."], "expected_facts": ["$4.00", "$4.50"]}
{"id": "edge-027", "question": "I'm in the U.S. on humanitarian parole. Does MST have a discounted fare category for me?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nYes. MST lists \"Special Humanitarian Parole\" as a discount fare category [doc:mst-fares]. \n\nThe published policy does not specify what documentation is required to prove humanitarian parole status or how to obtain an MST Courtesy Card under this category. For details on how to apply and what to bring, please contact MST directly at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or visit mst.org/fares/.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm in the U.S. on humanitarian parole. Does MST have a discounted fare category for me?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>Yes. MST lists \"Special Humanitarian Parole\" as a discount fare category [mst-fares].</p><p>The published policy does not specify what documentation is required to prove humanitarian parole status or how to obtain an MST Courtesy Card under this category. For details on how to apply and what to bring, please contact MST directly at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or visit mst.org/fares/.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "Courtesy Cards are available to all disabled individuals free of charge with a physician’s written verification or a Medicare Card. Use the card for discounted fares and passes as proof of eligibility. Obtain an application in person at the MST Bus Stop Shop, 201 Pearl Street in Monterey, Marina Transit Exchange, 280 Reservation Road in Marina, Salinas Transit Center, 110 Salinas Street in Salinas, or here ( English application / Spanish application ).", "MST accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets onboard all buses – and with fare capping, you will never be charged more than $6 per day, $20 per week, or $70 per month no matter how many times you ride in a day—as long as you pay with the same contactless debit/credit card or mobile wallet each time you ride. (visit Benefits page for details about tapping and discounted fares)\nTransfers If you need to transfer and are paying with cash or a GoPass, request a 2-hour pass from your driver when boarding your first bus.\nIf you are paying with a contactless payment method, simply tap it on the contactless payment reader on each bus you board and you will only be charged $2 no matter how many times you tap and transfer within that 2-hour window.", "MST now accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets. At this time, accepted mobile wallets include: Apple Pay , Google Pay, Samsung Pay , and Fitbit Pay . For more information, visit our Contactless Fare Payment Fast Facts and FAQs .\nCash\nCash is inserted into the farebox. Exact fare is not required. If you do not have exact fare the farebox will issue credit for use on future MST trips.", "MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month."], "expected_facts": ["Humanitarian Parole"]}
{"id": "edge-028", "question": "Can I get the SBMTD reduced-fare ID with my DMV disabled placard receipt?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nYes, according to SBMTD's published policy, you may present a valid California DMV Disabled Placard Receipt to obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card. [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]\n\nTo proceed, contact MTD at 805.963.3366 for more information, or visit the MTD Transit Center to apply.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Can I get the SBMTD reduced-fare ID with my DMV disabled placard receipt?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>Yes, according to SBMTD's published policy, you may present a valid California DMV Disabled Placard Receipt to obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card. [sbmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>To proceed, contact MTD at 805.963.3366 for more information, or visit the MTD Transit Center to apply.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Passengers with disabilities may be eligible for the Mobility Pass, a reduced fare to ride MTD buses.\nTo obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card, passengers must have an application completed by a qualified physician or an MTD-certified social service agency. You may download an application here , or get one at the MTD Transit Center, or call 805.963.3366 for more information.\nPassengers may also present a disabled photo ID card from another transit agency, a valid Medicare ID card, or a valid California DMV Disabled Placard Receipt to obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card.\nIf the photo ID card is lost, a replacement fee of $5.00 will be charged.\nUCSB & SBCC STUDENTS RIDE FREE\nUCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.", "All fares are one-way and may be paid with coins, bills, passes, or via Tap2Ride with a contactless bank card or mobile wallet. Exact change required when paying with cash; fareboxes do not give change and drivers cannot make change.\nYou can take advantage of pay-as-you-go discounted daily and monthly pass rates when you Tap2Ride.\nClick here to learn more, including how to get a contactless bank card or to add a bank card to the mobile wallet on your smart device.\nStandard\n$2.50 Regular one-way Youth (K-12th grade)\n$1.25 Seniors (age 65+) Persons with Disabilities* Persons with Medicare Cards* (Cash or Tap2Ride **) *Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\nFREE Children under 45 inches tall (three children per adult) Transfers (to complete a one-way trip) For more information on Transfers, click here .\n$6.00 Day Pass (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$2.50 Token for one-way fare", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap.", "UCSB and SBCC Student Bus Pass Programs : All UCSB & SBCC students pay a transportation fee and may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.\nDiscounted Fare Programs: Individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth are all eligible for reduced fares. Discounts are also available for pre-paid passes including Day passes, 10 – Ride passes, and 30 – Day passes.\nFare Capping: Pay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet. You can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nFree Transfers: If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus. Transfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. Free transfers work as follows: upon request, riders can receive a free 60 minute transfer to be used to board a second bus, heading in the same direction (i.e., an outbound Line 20 to an outbound Line 6). Riders may also receive a transfer on that second bus that is good for 30 minutes, also headed in the same direction. You can find more information on free transfers here .\nOther Transfers: With a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!\nPacific Surfliner’s Transit Transfer Program: On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.\nAlternative Funding Sources: MTD is actively seeking new funding sources. MTD continues to apply to grant programs to operate and improve service, maintain and replace vehicles, and enhance transit facilities throughout the district.", "10-RIDE PASS Discounted\n$17.50 Adult\n$12.50 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$8.50 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* Apply here for persons with disabilities.\n*Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. MTD can accept cash, a local imprinted check, or a money order (payable to “MTD”) as payment for passes.\n30-DAY PASS Discounted\n$80.00 Adult (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$65.00 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$30.00 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* (purchased in advance or via Tap2Ride**) * Apply here for a physical pass for persons with disabilities. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\n1-DAY PASS\nSystemwide Day Pass $6.00 (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride) Good on all routes. Valid for unlimited trips in one calendar day.\n30-Day Passes are valid for 30 days from first use. 30-Day passes are not transferable and transfers are not available with this pass.\n10-Ride Passes do not expire.\nImproper use of passes may result in revocation or confiscation.\nAll pass sales are final. No refunds. Passes have no cash value.", "Efficiency and Faster Boarding: Pre-paid fare systems allow passengers to pay before boarding, reducing the time buses need to spend at bus stops and instead getting you where you need to go.\nImproved Passenger Flow: Pre-paid systems contribute to smoother passenger flow on and off the bus, reducing crowding and making the overall transit experience more pleasant for everyone.\nCost Savings: Handling cash transactions can be resource intensive. Moving to a contactless payment system or pre-paid system can reduce the costs associated with cash handling services.\nFare Capping: Prepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here ."], "expected_facts": ["Placard"]}
{"id": "edge-029", "question": "I'm 62 — do I get the reduced fare on Humboldt Transit?", "provenance": {"source": "HTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-17:\nThe published criteria for reduced fares on Humboldt Transit include seniors aged 62+.\n[doc:hta-fares] However, the policy does not specify how you document or prove your age to the driver.\n**How to use the reduced fare:**\nReduced fares are available only with Stored Value passes ($10 or $20).\n[doc:hta-fares] Each ride will be deducted at $1 instead of $2.\n[doc:hta-fares] These passes work on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems and on Dial-A-Ride, but not on Flex microtransit.\n[doc:hta-fares]\nYou can purchase Stored Value passes using the Token Transit app, at the HTA office (133 V Street, Eureka), online, or on the bus with exact cash.\n[doc:hta-fares]\nTo confirm what documentation the driver will accept to verify your age, contact Humboldt Transit Authority at 707-443-0826.\n[doc:hta-fares]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["hta-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm 62 — do I get the reduced fare on Humboldt Transit?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-17:</p><p>The published criteria for reduced fares on Humboldt Transit include seniors aged 62+.</p><p>[hta-fares] However, the policy does not specify how you document or prove your age to the driver.</p><p>**How to use the reduced fare:**</p><p>Reduced fares are available only with Stored Value passes ($10 or $20).</p><p>[hta-fares] Each ride will be deducted at $1 instead of $2.</p><p>[hta-fares] These passes work on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems and on Dial-A-Ride, but not on Flex microtransit.</p><p>[hta-fares]</p><p>You can purchase Stored Value passes using the Token Transit app, at the HTA office (133 V Street, Eureka), online, or on the bus with exact cash.</p><p>[hta-fares]</p><p>To confirm what documentation the driver will accept to verify your age, contact Humboldt Transit Authority at 707-443-0826.</p><p>[hta-fares]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>HTA: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Insert the pass when you board and the value of each ride will be deducted. The amount deducted will be either:\n$2 (all rides unless eligible for a reduced fare)\n$1 (all rides if eligible for a reduced fare)\nThis is the only pass eligible for reduced fares. Let your driver know if you are entitled to a reduced fare before your insert your pass.\nReduced Fare Eligibility Requirements:\nSeniors Aged 62+\nYouth Aged 3-17\nPeople with a disabled person Transit ID Card\nThese passes work on all routes except Flex microtransit.", "Cal Poly Humboldt students , faculty, and staff can ride A&MRTS, RTS, ETS, SH, NSE 299, and NSE 101 using their ID cards (Flex microtransit, and Dial-A-Ride routes are not included)!\nStudents : Just show and swipe your current student ID when you board. All of your rides are free!\nStaff & Faculty : you can buy into the Jack Pass ID program for $60 per semester. Get more info from HSU Parking & Commuter Services .\nQuestions?\nIf you have any questions call Humboldt Transit Authority at 707-443-0826.", "The Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems are:\nRedwood Transit Service (RTS)\nEureka Transit Service (ETS)\nNorth State Express: Route 299: (NSE 299) [This system was formerly called Willow Creek (WC)\nSouthern Humboldt (SH)\nNorth State Express: Route 101 (NSE 101) [This system was formerly called Redwood Coast Express]\nArcata & Mad River Transit System (A&MRTS)\nThe Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems are:\nDial-A-Ride\nFlex microtransit\nTicket Exclusions may apply.\nLook for the Ride Humboldt logo on the bus:", "Single Ride, Day, Week, and Month passes are accepted on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems. They are not accepted on any Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems.\nStored Value passes ($10 or $20) are accepted on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems, and on Dial-A-Ride. They are not accepted on Flex microtransit.\nTicket Exclusion details", "Purchase virtual passes anytime and anywhere on your phone using the Token Transit app ! Pay with your credit, debit, or commuter benefits card and always have your pass with you.\nYou can also purchase physical passes at the HTA online store with a credit card and have them mailed to you via the US Postal Service.\nBus drivers sell passes on the bus, but only cash is accepted. You must have the exact amount as the drivers do not provide change. Important note: If you are not paying a cash fare do not put your money in the farebox until you tell the driver what you want to buy. You will not be refunded for money placed in farebox accidently.\nPasses can also be purchased at the Humboldt Transit Authority office at 133 V Street in Eureka using cash, Visa or Mastercard debit or credit card, or a personal check.", "Pay for fares with your contactless credit or debit (Visa or Mastercard) card, or payment enabled device.\nNo bus ticket needed!\nWhen using the same payment card, enjoy automatic fare capping equal to our Day, Week, and Month Pass rates.\nReduced fares are not eligible with this payment method.\nSee link for more details."], "expected_facts": ["62"]}
{"id": "edge-030", "question": "Can I get the senior discount on a Humboldt Transit monthly pass?", "provenance": {"source": "HTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-17:\nNo.\nAccording to the published policy, reduced fares are not eligible with Day, Week, and Month Passes.\n[doc:hta-fares] The only pass type eligible for reduced fares on Humboldt Transit is the Stored Value pass ($10 or $20), which deducts $1 per ride if you are eligible for a reduced fare.\n[doc:hta-fares]\nSeniors aged 62+ are listed among the published reduced-fare eligibility criteria.\n[doc:hta-fares]\nFor questions about your specific situation, you can contact Humboldt Transit Authority at 707-443-0826.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["hta-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Can I get the senior discount on a Humboldt Transit monthly pass?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-17:</p><p>No.</p><p>According to the published policy, reduced fares are not eligible with Day, Week, and Month Passes.</p><p>[hta-fares] The only pass type eligible for reduced fares on Humboldt Transit is the Stored Value pass ($10 or $20), which deducts $1 per ride if you are eligible for a reduced fare.</p><p>[hta-fares]</p><p>Seniors aged 62+ are listed among the published reduced-fare eligibility criteria.</p><p>[hta-fares]</p><p>For questions about your specific situation, you can contact Humboldt Transit Authority at 707-443-0826.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>HTA: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Cal Poly Humboldt students , faculty, and staff can ride A&MRTS, RTS, ETS, SH, NSE 299, and NSE 101 using their ID cards (Flex microtransit, and Dial-A-Ride routes are not included)!\nStudents : Just show and swipe your current student ID when you board. All of your rides are free!\nStaff & Faculty : you can buy into the Jack Pass ID program for $60 per semester. Get more info from HSU Parking & Commuter Services .\nQuestions?\nIf you have any questions call Humboldt Transit Authority at 707-443-0826.", "The Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems are:\nRedwood Transit Service (RTS)\nEureka Transit Service (ETS)\nNorth State Express: Route 299: (NSE 299) [This system was formerly called Willow Creek (WC)\nSouthern Humboldt (SH)\nNorth State Express: Route 101 (NSE 101) [This system was formerly called Redwood Coast Express]\nArcata & Mad River Transit System (A&MRTS)\nThe Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems are:\nDial-A-Ride\nFlex microtransit\nTicket Exclusions may apply.\nLook for the Ride Humboldt logo on the bus:", "Purchase virtual passes anytime and anywhere on your phone using the Token Transit app ! Pay with your credit, debit, or commuter benefits card and always have your pass with you.\nYou can also purchase physical passes at the HTA online store with a credit card and have them mailed to you via the US Postal Service.\nBus drivers sell passes on the bus, but only cash is accepted. You must have the exact amount as the drivers do not provide change. Important note: If you are not paying a cash fare do not put your money in the farebox until you tell the driver what you want to buy. You will not be refunded for money placed in farebox accidently.\nPasses can also be purchased at the Humboldt Transit Authority office at 133 V Street in Eureka using cash, Visa or Mastercard debit or credit card, or a personal check.", "Single Ride, Day, Week, and Month passes are accepted on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems. They are not accepted on any Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems.\nStored Value passes ($10 or $20) are accepted on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems, and on Dial-A-Ride. They are not accepted on Flex microtransit.\nTicket Exclusion details", "Insert the pass when you board and the value of each ride will be deducted. The amount deducted will be either:\n$2 (all rides unless eligible for a reduced fare)\n$1 (all rides if eligible for a reduced fare)\nThis is the only pass eligible for reduced fares. Let your driver know if you are entitled to a reduced fare before your insert your pass.\nReduced Fare Eligibility Requirements:\nSeniors Aged 62+\nYouth Aged 3-17\nPeople with a disabled person Transit ID Card\nThese passes work on all routes except Flex microtransit.", "Free transfers are only provided between ETS routes for a single trip. No inter-agency transfers are available.\nTell the driver you want a transfer when you board the bus. You can only get a transfer upon boarding.\nTo use a transfer when you board a bus on another route simply insert it into the card reader. When you hear a beep the transfer is recognized as valid and you may proceed to board the bus."], "expected_facts": ["Stored Value"]}
{"id": "edge-031", "question": "My 16-year-old rides Humboldt Transit — do they get a youth discount?", "provenance": {"source": "HTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-17:\nYes, youth aged 3–17 may qualify for a reduced fare on Humboldt Transit.\n[doc:hta-fares]\n**How it works:**\nReduced fares apply only to **Stored Value passes** ($10 or $20).\nWith a Stored Value pass, each ride costs $1 instead of the regular $2 if you are eligible for a reduced fare.\n[doc:hta-fares]\n**What you need to do:**\n[doc:hta-fares]\n**Where to buy:**\nYou can purchase Stored Value passes:\n- On your phone using the Token Transit app (pay with credit, debit, or commuter benefits card)\n- At the HTA online store (credit card, mailed via US Postal Service)\n- On the bus from the driver (cash only, exact amount required)\n- At the Humboldt Transit Authority office at 133 V Street in Eureka (cash, Visa, Mastercard, or personal check) [doc:hta-fares]\nReduced fares do **not** apply to Day, Week, or Month passes.\n[doc:hta-fares]\nFor any questions, you can call Humboldt Transit Authority at 707-443-0826.\n[doc:hta-fares]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["hta-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>My 16-year-old rides Humboldt Transit — do they get a youth discount?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-17:</p><p>Yes, youth aged 3–17 may qualify for a reduced fare on Humboldt Transit.</p><p>[hta-fares]</p><p>**How it works:**</p><p>Reduced fares apply only to **Stored Value passes** ($10 or $20).</p><p>With a Stored Value pass, each ride costs $1 instead of the regular $2 if you are eligible for a reduced fare.</p><p>[hta-fares]</p><p>**What you need to do:**</p><p>[hta-fares]</p><p>**Where to buy:**</p><p>You can purchase Stored Value passes:</p><p>- On your phone using the Token Transit app (pay with credit, debit, or commuter benefits card)</p><p>- At the HTA online store (credit card, mailed via US Postal Service)</p><p>- On the bus from the driver (cash only, exact amount required)</p><p>- At the Humboldt Transit Authority office at 133 V Street in Eureka (cash, Visa, Mastercard, or personal check) [hta-fares]</p><p>Reduced fares do **not** apply to Day, Week, or Month passes.</p><p>[hta-fares]</p><p>For any questions, you can call Humboldt Transit Authority at 707-443-0826.</p><p>[hta-fares]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>HTA: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Single Ride, Day, Week, and Month passes are accepted on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems. They are not accepted on any Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems.\nStored Value passes ($10 or $20) are accepted on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems, and on Dial-A-Ride. They are not accepted on Flex microtransit.\nTicket Exclusion details", "Cal Poly Humboldt students , faculty, and staff can ride A&MRTS, RTS, ETS, SH, NSE 299, and NSE 101 using their ID cards (Flex microtransit, and Dial-A-Ride routes are not included)!\nStudents : Just show and swipe your current student ID when you board. All of your rides are free!\nStaff & Faculty : you can buy into the Jack Pass ID program for $60 per semester. Get more info from HSU Parking & Commuter Services .\nQuestions?\nIf you have any questions call Humboldt Transit Authority at 707-443-0826.", "The Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems are:\nRedwood Transit Service (RTS)\nEureka Transit Service (ETS)\nNorth State Express: Route 299: (NSE 299) [This system was formerly called Willow Creek (WC)\nSouthern Humboldt (SH)\nNorth State Express: Route 101 (NSE 101) [This system was formerly called Redwood Coast Express]\nArcata & Mad River Transit System (A&MRTS)\nThe Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems are:\nDial-A-Ride\nFlex microtransit\nTicket Exclusions may apply.\nLook for the Ride Humboldt logo on the bus:", "Insert the pass when you board and the value of each ride will be deducted. The amount deducted will be either:\n$2 (all rides unless eligible for a reduced fare)\n$1 (all rides if eligible for a reduced fare)\nThis is the only pass eligible for reduced fares. Let your driver know if you are entitled to a reduced fare before your insert your pass.\nReduced Fare Eligibility Requirements:\nSeniors Aged 62+\nYouth Aged 3-17\nPeople with a disabled person Transit ID Card\nThese passes work on all routes except Flex microtransit.", "Purchase virtual passes anytime and anywhere on your phone using the Token Transit app ! Pay with your credit, debit, or commuter benefits card and always have your pass with you.\nYou can also purchase physical passes at the HTA online store with a credit card and have them mailed to you via the US Postal Service.\nBus drivers sell passes on the bus, but only cash is accepted. You must have the exact amount as the drivers do not provide change. Important note: If you are not paying a cash fare do not put your money in the farebox until you tell the driver what you want to buy. You will not be refunded for money placed in farebox accidently.\nPasses can also be purchased at the Humboldt Transit Authority office at 133 V Street in Eureka using cash, Visa or Mastercard debit or credit card, or a personal check.", "Cash | Tap-to-Pay\nSingle Ride | $2.00\n2 Hour Unlimited Rides | $2.00\n1 Day Pass | $5.00 | $5.00\n7 Day Pass | $15.00 | $15.00\n31 Day Pass | $50.00 | $50.00\nStored Value Passes\nMedia | Per Ride Regular | Per Ride Reduced\n$10 Stored Value Pass | $2.00 | $1.00\n$20 Stored Value Pass | $2.00 | $1.00"], "expected_facts": ["3–17"]}
{"id": "edge-032", "question": "Do Cal Poly Humboldt students ride free on Humboldt Transit?", "provenance": {"source": "HTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-17:\n\nYes. Cal Poly Humboldt students can ride free on Humboldt Transit. According to the published policy, students \"just show and swipe your current student ID when you board. All of your rides are free!\" [doc:hta-fares]\n\nThis applies to the fixed-route systems: A&MRTS, RTS, ETS, SH, NSE 299, and NSE 101. Flex microtransit and Dial-A-Ride routes are not included. [doc:hta-fares]\n\nFor any questions, you can contact Humboldt Transit Authority at 707-443-0826.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["hta-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Do Cal Poly Humboldt students ride free on Humboldt Transit?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-17:</p><p>Yes. Cal Poly Humboldt students can ride free on Humboldt Transit. According to the published policy, students \"just show and swipe your current student ID when you board. All of your rides are free!\" [hta-fares]</p><p>This applies to the fixed-route systems: A&amp;MRTS, RTS, ETS, SH, NSE 299, and NSE 101. Flex microtransit and Dial-A-Ride routes are not included. [hta-fares]</p><p>For any questions, you can contact Humboldt Transit Authority at 707-443-0826.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>HTA: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Cal Poly Humboldt students , faculty, and staff can ride A&MRTS, RTS, ETS, SH, NSE 299, and NSE 101 using their ID cards (Flex microtransit, and Dial-A-Ride routes are not included)!\nStudents : Just show and swipe your current student ID when you board. All of your rides are free!\nStaff & Faculty : you can buy into the Jack Pass ID program for $60 per semester. Get more info from HSU Parking & Commuter Services .\nQuestions?\nIf you have any questions call Humboldt Transit Authority at 707-443-0826.", "The Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems are:\nRedwood Transit Service (RTS)\nEureka Transit Service (ETS)\nNorth State Express: Route 299: (NSE 299) [This system was formerly called Willow Creek (WC)\nSouthern Humboldt (SH)\nNorth State Express: Route 101 (NSE 101) [This system was formerly called Redwood Coast Express]\nArcata & Mad River Transit System (A&MRTS)\nThe Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems are:\nDial-A-Ride\nFlex microtransit\nTicket Exclusions may apply.\nLook for the Ride Humboldt logo on the bus:", "Single Ride, Day, Week, and Month passes are accepted on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems. They are not accepted on any Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems.\nStored Value passes ($10 or $20) are accepted on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems, and on Dial-A-Ride. They are not accepted on Flex microtransit.\nTicket Exclusion details", "Purchase virtual passes anytime and anywhere on your phone using the Token Transit app ! Pay with your credit, debit, or commuter benefits card and always have your pass with you.\nYou can also purchase physical passes at the HTA online store with a credit card and have them mailed to you via the US Postal Service.\nBus drivers sell passes on the bus, but only cash is accepted. You must have the exact amount as the drivers do not provide change. Important note: If you are not paying a cash fare do not put your money in the farebox until you tell the driver what you want to buy. You will not be refunded for money placed in farebox accidently.\nPasses can also be purchased at the Humboldt Transit Authority office at 133 V Street in Eureka using cash, Visa or Mastercard debit or credit card, or a personal check.", "Insert the pass when you board and the value of each ride will be deducted. The amount deducted will be either:\n$2 (all rides unless eligible for a reduced fare)\n$1 (all rides if eligible for a reduced fare)\nThis is the only pass eligible for reduced fares. Let your driver know if you are entitled to a reduced fare before your insert your pass.\nReduced Fare Eligibility Requirements:\nSeniors Aged 62+\nYouth Aged 3-17\nPeople with a disabled person Transit ID Card\nThese passes work on all routes except Flex microtransit.", "Cash | Tap-to-Pay\nSingle Ride | $2.00\n2 Hour Unlimited Rides | $2.00\n1 Day Pass | $5.00 | $5.00\n7 Day Pass | $15.00 | $15.00\n31 Day Pass | $50.00 | $50.00\nStored Value Passes\nMedia | Per Ride Regular | Per Ride Reduced\n$10 Stored Value Pass | $2.00 | $1.00\n$20 Stored Value Pass | $2.00 | $1.00"], "expected_facts": ["free"]}
{"id": "edge-033", "question": "Is the senior fare age the same on Humboldt Transit and MST?", "provenance": {"source": "HTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-17:\n\nNo, the senior fare ages differ between the two agencies.\n\n**MST** offers a discount fare for people **65 years and older** [doc:mst-fares].\n\n**Humboldt Transit Authority** offers reduced fares for **seniors aged 62+** [doc:hta-fares].\n\nSo HTA's senior reduced-fare eligibility begins at age 62, while MST's begins at age 65.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["hta-fares", "mst-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Is the senior fare age the same on Humboldt Transit and MST?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-17:</p><p>No, the senior fare ages differ between the two agencies.</p><p>**MST** offers a discount fare for people **65 years and older** [mst-fares].</p><p>**Humboldt Transit Authority** offers reduced fares for **seniors aged 62+** [hta-fares].</p><p>So HTA's senior reduced-fare eligibility begins at age 62, while MST's begins at age 65.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>HTA: Fares</li><li>MST: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "The Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems are:\nRedwood Transit Service (RTS)\nEureka Transit Service (ETS)\nNorth State Express: Route 299: (NSE 299) [This system was formerly called Willow Creek (WC)\nSouthern Humboldt (SH)\nNorth State Express: Route 101 (NSE 101) [This system was formerly called Redwood Coast Express]\nArcata & Mad River Transit System (A&MRTS)\nThe Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems are:\nDial-A-Ride\nFlex microtransit\nTicket Exclusions may apply.\nLook for the Ride Humboldt logo on the bus:", "Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "Cal Poly Humboldt students , faculty, and staff can ride A&MRTS, RTS, ETS, SH, NSE 299, and NSE 101 using their ID cards (Flex microtransit, and Dial-A-Ride routes are not included)!\nStudents : Just show and swipe your current student ID when you board. All of your rides are free!\nStaff & Faculty : you can buy into the Jack Pass ID program for $60 per semester. Get more info from HSU Parking & Commuter Services .\nQuestions?\nIf you have any questions call Humboldt Transit Authority at 707-443-0826.", "MST accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets onboard all buses – and with fare capping, you will never be charged more than $6 per day, $20 per week, or $70 per month no matter how many times you ride in a day—as long as you pay with the same contactless debit/credit card or mobile wallet each time you ride. (visit Benefits page for details about tapping and discounted fares)\nTransfers If you need to transfer and are paying with cash or a GoPass, request a 2-hour pass from your driver when boarding your first bus.\nIf you are paying with a contactless payment method, simply tap it on the contactless payment reader on each bus you board and you will only be charged $2 no matter how many times you tap and transfer within that 2-hour window.", "Insert the pass when you board and the value of each ride will be deducted. The amount deducted will be either:\n$2 (all rides unless eligible for a reduced fare)\n$1 (all rides if eligible for a reduced fare)\nThis is the only pass eligible for reduced fares. Let your driver know if you are entitled to a reduced fare before your insert your pass.\nReduced Fare Eligibility Requirements:\nSeniors Aged 62+\nYouth Aged 3-17\nPeople with a disabled person Transit ID Card\nThese passes work on all routes except Flex microtransit."], "expected_facts": ["62", "65"]}
{"id": "fresh-001", "question": "How current is your MST fare information?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12, the MST fare information I have comes from documents fetched on that date [doc:mst-fares], [doc:mst-fares-benefits], [doc:mst-veterans-resource], and [doc:mst-fares-es].\n\nFor anything time-sensitive—such as current pass prices, program eligibility details, or contact information—I recommend confirming directly with MST:\n- Phone: 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871)\n- Email: [email protected]\n- Website: mst.org", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares", "mst-fares-benefits", "mst-fares-es", "mst-veterans-resource"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How current is your MST fare information?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12, the MST fare information I have comes from documents fetched on that date [mst-fares], [mst-fares-benefits], [mst-veterans-resource], and [mst-fares-es].</p><p>For anything time-sensitive—such as current pass prices, program eligibility details, or contact information—I recommend confirming directly with MST:</p><p>- Phone: 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871)</p><p>- Email: [email protected]</p><p>- Website: mst.org</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li><li>MST: Benefits (Cal-ITP reduced fares)</li><li>MST: Tarifas (Spanish fares page)</li><li>MST: Veterans Resource</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST) is partnering with veterans’ services organizations to select individuals needing transportation services. Selected veterans are provided free MST passes to honor their service to our country. For information email [email protected]\nVeterans Group Travel Training\nMonterey-Salinas Transit (MST) provides free fixed-route travel training to teach interested individuals how to safely and independently ride the MST bus system.\nTravel Training", "MST accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets onboard all buses – and with fare capping, you will never be charged more than $6 per day, $20 per week, or $70 per month no matter how many times you ride in a day—as long as you pay with the same contactless debit/credit card or mobile wallet each time you ride. (visit Benefits page for details about tapping and discounted fares)\nTransfers If you need to transfer and are paying with cash or a GoPass, request a 2-hour pass from your driver when boarding your first bus.\nIf you are paying with a contactless payment method, simply tap it on the contactless payment reader on each bus you board and you will only be charged $2 no matter how many times you tap and transfer within that 2-hour window.", "For your convenience, MST GoPasses are available at MST Customer Service locations and pass outlet locations throughout your community.\nCarmel Carmel Drug Store, San Carlos & Ocean\nGonzales City Hall, 147 Fourth St.\nGreenfield City Hall, 45 El Camino Real\nKing City City Hall, 212 S. Vanderhurst Ave.\nMarina Marina Transit Exchange, 280 Reservation Road\nMonterey MST Bus Stop Shop, 201 Pearl Street\nSalinas Costco, 1339 N. Davis Rd. (Regular GoPass only) Salinas Transit Center, 110 Salinas Street\nSand City Costco, 801 Tioga Ave. (Regular GoPass only)\nSeaside Mal’s Market, 1264 Noche Buena\nGopasses and Gocards may also be purchased anytime online at the MST Store , by phone at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or by mail by sending your check, credit card information or money order to: GoPasses, 19 Upper Ragsdale Drive, Suite 200, Monterey, CA 93940.", "MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "For your convenience, MST GoPasses are available at MST Customer Service locations and pass outlet locations throughout your community.\nCarmel Carmel Drug Store, San Carlos & Ocean\nGonzales City Hall, 147 Fourth St.\nGreenfield City Hall, 45 El Camino Real\nKing City City Hall, 212 S. Vanderhurst Ave.\nMarina Marina Transit Exchange, 280 Reservation Road\nMonterey MST Bus Stop Shop, 201 Pearl Street\nSalinas Costco, 1339 N. Davis Rd. (Regular GoPass only) Salinas Transit Center, 110 Salinas Street\nSand City Costco, 801 Tioga Ave. (Regular GoPass only)\nSeaside Mal’s Market, 1264 Noche Buena\nGopasses and Gocards may also be purchased anytime online at the MST Store , by phone at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or by mail by sending your check, credit card information or money order to: GoPasses, 19 Upper Ragsdale Drive, Suite 200, Monterey, CA 93940.", "MST now accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets. At this time, accepted mobile wallets include: Apple Pay , Google Pay, Samsung Pay , and Fitbit Pay . For more information, visit our Contactless Fare Payment Fast Facts and FAQs .\nCash\nCash is inserted into the farebox. Exact fare is not required. If you do not have exact fare the farebox will issue credit for use on future MST trips."], "expected_facts": ["2026"]}
{"id": "fresh-002", "question": "Did SBMTD change its fares recently?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes, SBMTD changed its fares recently. The base fare was adjusted from $1.75 to $2.50 as of August 18, 2025 [doc:sbmtd-farechange]. This was the first fare increase in 16 years; the previous adjustment occurred on January 1, 2009 [doc:sbmtd-farechange]. The District made this change to keep pace with rising operating costs and to maintain service without reducing bus hours, frequency, or routes [doc:sbmtd-farechange].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12, I recommend confirming current fares with SBMTD directly at 805.963.3366 or visiting sbmtd.gov for any updates.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-farechange"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Did SBMTD change its fares recently?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes, SBMTD changed its fares recently. The base fare was adjusted from $1.75 to $2.50 as of August 18, 2025 [sbmtd-farechange]. This was the first fare increase in 16 years; the previous adjustment occurred on January 1, 2009 [sbmtd-farechange]. The District made this change to keep pace with rising operating costs and to maintain service without reducing bus hours, frequency, or routes [sbmtd-farechange].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12, I recommend confirming current fares with SBMTD directly at 805.963.3366 or visiting sbmtd.gov for any updates.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fare Change</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses.\nBudget Scenario Operating costs have increased by more than 10% since FY 2019. The average fare revenue collected per passenger has dropped from $1.11 to $1.07, still rebounding from the COVID pandemic. Fare revenue only accounts for 20% of MTD’s annual operating budget. The inflation rate for the fiscal year ending June 30, 2024 was 3.2%, indicating a trend for additional increases.\nThe major revenue sources for the District operations are fares, Federal Transit Administration (FTA) operating assistance, and sales tax. With the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Federal operating assistance has grown a little; however, the apportionment of funds is fixed and there doesn’t appear to be much of a change looking forward. Sales tax revenues experienced growth with inflationary price increases during the COVID pandemic; however, recently these increases have been leveling out.\nRevenue shortfalls and increasing operating expenditures in the areas of wages and benefits, fuel, insurance, and utilities are expected, continuing to increase the deficit. Without a fare adjustment, the District would be forced to reduce service.", "All fares are one-way and may be paid with coins, bills, passes, or via Tap2Ride with a contactless bank card or mobile wallet. Exact change required when paying with cash; fareboxes do not give change and drivers cannot make change.\nYou can take advantage of pay-as-you-go discounted daily and monthly pass rates when you Tap2Ride.\nClick here to learn more, including how to get a contactless bank card or to add a bank card to the mobile wallet on your smart device.\nStandard\n$2.50 Regular one-way Youth (K-12th grade)\n$1.25 Seniors (age 65+) Persons with Disabilities* Persons with Medicare Cards* (Cash or Tap2Ride **) *Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\nFREE Children under 45 inches tall (three children per adult) Transfers (to complete a one-way trip) For more information on Transfers, click here .\n$6.00 Day Pass (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$2.50 Token for one-way fare", "Santa Barbara Metropolitan Transit District (MTD) is committed to providing reliable and affordable transit services to our community. While MTD’s fares have not changed in 16 years, cost pressures have come to the point of requiring a change in fare structure. We have carefully reviewed our fare system, received community feedback, and conducted a fare equity analysis. After careful consideration we adjusted our base fare from the previous fare of $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nImplementation of this new fare structure is a significant step toward assuring MTD’s services are dependable and economically sustainable in the long term.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap.", "New fare structure for both cash & passes went into effect August 18, 2025. New passes became available on August 4, 2025. MTD will offer customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass. Old passes will continue to work through August 31, 2025. To see the exchange matrix for old to new passes, please click the button below.", "Efficiency and Faster Boarding: Pre-paid fare systems allow passengers to pay before boarding, reducing the time buses need to spend at bus stops and instead getting you where you need to go.\nImproved Passenger Flow: Pre-paid systems contribute to smoother passenger flow on and off the bus, reducing crowding and making the overall transit experience more pleasant for everyone.\nCost Savings: Handling cash transactions can be resource intensive. Moving to a contactless payment system or pre-paid system can reduce the costs associated with cash handling services.\nFare Capping: Prepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here ."], "expected_facts": ["August 18, 2025"]}
{"id": "fresh-003", "question": "Can I still use a ZipPass on SacRT?", "provenance": {"source": "SacRT published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nNo. SacRT's ZipPass app is no longer available for use. The published policy states: \"Last day to use passes/tickets: April 30, 2026\" [doc:sacrt-fares]. Since that deadline has passed, ZipPass is no longer valid.\n\nSacRT now offers two mobile fare apps instead:\n- **Transit Connect** — allows you to pre-purchase, store, and activate tickets and passes on your smartphone for bus or light rail [doc:sacrt-fares]\n- **Tap2Ride** — tap your contactless card, mobile device, or mobile wallet on Tap2Ride devices on bus and SacRT GO vehicles to pay fare [doc:sacrt-fares]\n\nYou can also purchase tickets and passes at light rail station fare vending machines or use the Connect Transit Card (a physical electronic card that arrives in 7–10 business days) [doc:sacrt-fares].\n\nFor the most current details, confirm with SacRT at sacrt.com or 511.org.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sacrt-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Can I still use a ZipPass on SacRT?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>No. SacRT's ZipPass app is no longer available for use. The published policy states: \"Last day to use passes/tickets: April 30, 2026\" [sacrt-fares]. Since that deadline has passed, ZipPass is no longer valid.</p><p>SacRT now offers two mobile fare apps instead:</p><p>- **Transit Connect** — allows you to pre-purchase, store, and activate tickets and passes on your smartphone for bus or light rail [sacrt-fares]</p><p>- **Tap2Ride** — tap your contactless card, mobile device, or mobile wallet on Tap2Ride devices on bus and SacRT GO vehicles to pay fare [sacrt-fares]</p><p>You can also purchase tickets and passes at light rail station fare vending machines or use the Connect Transit Card (a physical electronic card that arrives in 7–10 business days) [sacrt-fares].</p><p>For the most current details, confirm with SacRT at sacrt.com or 511.org.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SacRT: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["SacRT offers a variety of fare options to meet your travel needs.\nImportant Notice for ZipPass Users\nLast day to use passes/tickets: April 30, 2026", "Our mobile fare app ZipPass allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus or light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nNeed a ticket for your next SacRT Light Rail trip?\nOur fare vending machines located at all SacRT light rail stations are simple to use!\n1. Choose your ticket or pass\n2. Pick your quantity\n3. Complete your payment (cash or card)\n4. Take your ticket\nTap2Ride\nTap your contactless card, mobile device or mobile wallet on Tap2Ride devices on bus and SacRT GO vehicles to pay fare.", "SacRT offers fare-free transit for students in Transitional Kindergarten through 12th grade with the RydeFreeRT program and has a partnership with Los Rios and Sacramento State for college students to ride SacRT.\nCalifornia State Workers\nSEIU members have access to free transit through SacRT! Other state employee unions are eligible for transit subsidies to cover most of your commute costs.", "Connect Transit Card is the region's electronic transit fare payment system. You can now purchase your transit fare online, never have to carry exact change again AND use the card on more than one agency!\n1. Go through the \"get a card\" process\n2. Create an account and buy your transit fare\n3. Receive an email confirming your order\n4. Your Connect Transit Card arrives in 7-10 Business Days", "Our mobile fare app Transit Connect allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus and light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nLearn more at sacrt.com/TransitConnect .", "Single Ride Ticket\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$10.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$20.00\n*Discount (senior, disabled or student) monthly or semi-monthly stickers must be affixed to a valid SacRT photo ID, with the exception of the student stickers, which may be attached to a school issued photo ID.\n**Many students in grades TK through 12, are eligible for free transit passes. Check with your school district for details or visit RydeFreeRT.\nBus\nPassengers are required to pay a basic or discount single fare for each trip or may purchase a daily pass valid for unlimited rides on that day.\nLight Rail\nSingle ride tickets are valid for 90 minutes from time of validation on light rail only, or pay the 25 cent transfer fee when boarding a bus.\nFare Accepted by Other Transit Agencies\nYour SacRT ticket or pass maybe eligible to transfer to and from other transit agencies. For more detailed fare info or a list of SacRT fares accepted by other transit agencies, click here ."], "expected_facts": ["April 30, 2026"]}
{"id": "fresh-004", "question": "How long are the current Yolobus fares in effect?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nThe Yolobus fixed route bus fares are effective **July 1, 2025 – June 30, 2026** [doc:yolobus-fares].\n\nThe passages do not specify the effective dates for ADA paratransit fares, daily passes, or monthly passes. For those details, contact Yolobus directly at (530) 661-0816 or visit yolobus.com.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How long are the current Yolobus fares in effect?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>The Yolobus fixed route bus fares are effective **July 1, 2025 – June 30, 2026** [yolobus-fares].</p><p>The passages do not specify the effective dates for ADA paratransit fares, daily passes, or monthly passes. For those details, contact Yolobus directly at (530) 661-0816 or visit yolobus.com.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Yolobus: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["ADA-eligible riders can use ADA paratransit (“Yolobus Special”) service. The Yolobus Special fares vary by distance (local vs. intercity).\nADA Paratransit (\"Yolobus Special\") Products\nADA Paratransit Local Fare | $4.00\nADA Paratransit Intercity Fare | $4.50\nADA Paratransit Premium Fare* | $6.00\n* Premium service is available to medical facilities only in portions of Sacramento and Vacaville that are beyond ¾ miles of Yolobus fixed routes. Destinations within ¾ mile of a Yolobus fixed route are charged the Local or Intercity fare, whichever is applicable\nHow to purchase a ticket or a pass\nMore information on how to purchase a ticket or a pass is available here", "Riders have many choices when paying their fares. While fares can be paid onboard the bus with cash, contactless payment is more efficient and reduces bus boarding times—getting riders to their destinations faster. Contactless fare payment options include the Connect Card and the Transit Connect mobile app.", "Riders have many choices when paying their fares. Fares may be paid on board the bus with cash, but contactless payment is more efficient and reduces bus boarding times—getting riders to their destinations faster. Contactless fare payment options include Connect Card and the Transit Connect mobile app.", "Yolobus fixed route bus fares vary depending on which type of service you ride. All below fares are effective July 1, 2025 – June 30, 2026 .", "Our senior (62+) and disabled riders can take advantage of Yolobus’ reduced fares. To qualify for reduced fares, riders must show proper identification when purchasing fares and to bus operators when boarding.\nQualifying riders may obtain a reduced fare photo ID by visiting the Yolo Transportation District office or SacRT Customer Service & Sales Center:\nYolo Transportation District\n350 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776 (Mon-Thu, 9:00 AM-Noon, 1:00-4:00 PM)\n(530) 661-0816", "Yolobus-only and Sacramento Regional Transit Monthly Passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown, and through the first business day of the following month. Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or on the Transit Connect app, but not onboard a Yolobus."], "expected_facts": ["June 30, 2026"]}
{"id": "fresh-005", "question": "Are the fare prices you quote guaranteed to be what I'll pay tomorrow?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-17, I cannot guarantee that the fares I quote will be what you pay tomorrow. Transit agencies adjust fares periodically, and the passages I have access to reflect only the policies current as of that date.\n\nFor example, Santa Barbara MTD adjusted its base fare from $1.75 to $2.50 as of August 18, 2025 [doc:sbmtd-farechange], and the passages note that \"fares have not kept pace with\" rising operating costs [doc:sbmtd-farechange]. This shows that fare changes do occur.\n\nTo confirm current fares for any of the five agencies covered here (Monterey-Salinas Transit, Santa Barbara MTD, Yolobus, Sacramento Regional Transit, or Humboldt Transit Authority), I recommend:\n\n- Visiting the agency's official website\n- Calling the agency directly\n- Checking 511.org for real-time transit information\n\nThis is especially important if you are planning a trip or making a purchasing decision.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-farechange"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Are the fare prices you quote guaranteed to be what I'll pay tomorrow?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-17, I cannot guarantee that the fares I quote will be what you pay tomorrow. Transit agencies adjust fares periodically, and the passages I have access to reflect only the policies current as of that date.</p><p>For example, Santa Barbara MTD adjusted its base fare from $1.75 to $2.50 as of August 18, 2025 [sbmtd-farechange], and the passages note that \"fares have not kept pace with\" rising operating costs [sbmtd-farechange]. This shows that fare changes do occur.</p><p>To confirm current fares for any of the five agencies covered here (Monterey-Salinas Transit, Santa Barbara MTD, Yolobus, Sacramento Regional Transit, or Humboldt Transit Authority), I recommend:</p><p>- Visiting the agency's official website</p><p>- Calling the agency directly</p><p>- Checking 511.org for real-time transit information</p><p>This is especially important if you are planning a trip or making a purchasing decision.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fare Change</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Purchase virtual passes anytime and anywhere on your phone using the Token Transit app ! Pay with your credit, debit, or commuter benefits card and always have your pass with you.\nYou can also purchase physical passes at the HTA online store with a credit card and have them mailed to you via the US Postal Service.\nBus drivers sell passes on the bus, but only cash is accepted. You must have the exact amount as the drivers do not provide change. Important note: If you are not paying a cash fare do not put your money in the farebox until you tell the driver what you want to buy. You will not be refunded for money placed in farebox accidently.\nPasses can also be purchased at the Humboldt Transit Authority office at 133 V Street in Eureka using cash, Visa or Mastercard debit or credit card, or a personal check.", "Santa Barbara Metropolitan Transit District (MTD) is committed to providing reliable and affordable transit services to our community. While MTD’s fares have not changed in 16 years, cost pressures have come to the point of requiring a change in fare structure. We have carefully reviewed our fare system, received community feedback, and conducted a fare equity analysis. After careful consideration we adjusted our base fare from the previous fare of $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nImplementation of this new fare structure is a significant step toward assuring MTD’s services are dependable and economically sustainable in the long term.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap.", "The table below shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD’s goal with this fare adjustment is to restore a portion of the approximately $3.0 million in annual FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities operating assistance that was lost when the 2020 Census found that the population of the Santa Barbara urbanized area exceeds 200,000 persons. The fare adjustment is estimated to generate approximately $1.5 million in increased revenue annually.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses.\nBudget Scenario Operating costs have increased by more than 10% since FY 2019. The average fare revenue collected per passenger has dropped from $1.11 to $1.07, still rebounding from the COVID pandemic. Fare revenue only accounts for 20% of MTD’s annual operating budget. The inflation rate for the fiscal year ending June 30, 2024 was 3.2%, indicating a trend for additional increases.\nThe major revenue sources for the District operations are fares, Federal Transit Administration (FTA) operating assistance, and sales tax. With the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Federal operating assistance has grown a little; however, the apportionment of funds is fixed and there doesn’t appear to be much of a change looking forward. Sales tax revenues experienced growth with inflationary price increases during the COVID pandemic; however, recently these increases have been leveling out.\nRevenue shortfalls and increasing operating expenditures in the areas of wages and benefits, fuel, insurance, and utilities are expected, continuing to increase the deficit. Without a fare adjustment, the District would be forced to reduce service.", "UCSB and SBCC Student Bus Pass Programs : All UCSB & SBCC students pay a transportation fee and may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.\nDiscounted Fare Programs: Individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth are all eligible for reduced fares. Discounts are also available for pre-paid passes including Day passes, 10 – Ride passes, and 30 – Day passes.\nFare Capping: Pay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet. You can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nFree Transfers: If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus. Transfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. Free transfers work as follows: upon request, riders can receive a free 60 minute transfer to be used to board a second bus, heading in the same direction (i.e., an outbound Line 20 to an outbound Line 6). Riders may also receive a transfer on that second bus that is good for 30 minutes, also headed in the same direction. You can find more information on free transfers here .\nOther Transfers: With a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!\nPacific Surfliner’s Transit Transfer Program: On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.\nAlternative Funding Sources: MTD is actively seeking new funding sources. MTD continues to apply to grant programs to operate and improve service, maintain and replace vehicles, and enhance transit facilities throughout the district."]}
{"id": "fresh-006", "question": "What will MST fares be next year?", "provenance": {"source": "Monterey-Salinas Transit published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "I don't have a published policy document that answers that, and I won't guess about fares or eligibility. Please check your transit agency directly, or https://511.org (Bay Area) or the agency's own website for current information.", "refused": true, "language": "en", "citations": []}, "should_refuse": true, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What will MST fares be next year?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>I don't have a published policy document that answers that, and I won't guess about fares or eligibility. Please check your transit agency directly, or https://511.org (Bay Area) or the agency's own website for current information.</p></section>"}
{"id": "fresh-007", "question": "When does Transit Connect start working on Unitrans?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on the passages provided, Transit Connect is scheduled to join in **summer 2026** [doc:yolobus-fares, doc:yolobus-purchasing]. The published policy does not specify an exact date within that window.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12, I recommend confirming the precise launch date with Unitrans or Yolobus directly, as summer timelines can vary.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares", "yolobus-purchasing"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>When does Transit Connect start working on Unitrans?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on the passages provided, Transit Connect is scheduled to join in **summer 2026** [yolobus-fares, doc:yolobus-purchasing]. The published policy does not specify an exact date within that window.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12, I recommend confirming the precise launch date with Unitrans or Yolobus directly, as summer timelines can vary.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Yolobus: Fares</li><li>Yolobus: Purchasing a Ticket or Pass</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["1225 R Street, Sacramento, CA 95811 (Mon-Fri, 8:30 AM-5:00 PM)\n(916) 321-2877\nOther forms of IDs that qualify for a discount fare includes:\nMedicare Card\nCalifornia Driver’s License\nCalifornia ID Card\nOther transits agencies’ Disabled ID (such as Unitrans, SacRT, etc.)", "The Transit Connect mobile fare app allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone. Accepted on Yolobus and SacRT, with Unitrans joining in summer 2026.", "The Transit Connect mobile fare app allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone. Accepted on Yolobus and SacRT, with Unitrans joining in summer 2026. Download the free app from the App Store or Google Play.", "Riders have many choices when paying their fares. Fares may be paid on board the bus with cash, but contactless payment is more efficient and reduces bus boarding times—getting riders to their destinations faster. Contactless fare payment options include Connect Card and the Transit Connect mobile app.", "Riders have many choices when paying their fares. While fares can be paid onboard the bus with cash, contactless payment is more efficient and reduces bus boarding times—getting riders to their destinations faster. Contactless fare payment options include the Connect Card and the Transit Connect mobile app.", "Connect Card is the Sacramento region’s electronic transit fare payment system and is accepted by most transit agencies in the Sacramento region. Connect Card readers are installed on all Yolobus vehicles. To pay, tag the Connect Card reader with your card upon boarding the bus."], "expected_facts": ["summer 2026"]}
{"id": "fresh-008", "question": "Is the SBMTD Line 16 shuttle still suspended?", "provenance": {"source": "Santa Barbara Metropolitan Transit District published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "I don't have a published policy document that answers that, and I won't guess about fares or eligibility. Please check your transit agency directly, or https://511.org (Bay Area) or the agency's own website for current information.", "refused": true, "language": "en", "citations": []}, "should_refuse": true, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Is the SBMTD Line 16 shuttle still suspended?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>I don't have a published policy document that answers that, and I won't guess about fares or eligibility. Please check your transit agency directly, or https://511.org (Bay Area) or the agency's own website for current information.</p></section>"}
{"id": "fresh-009", "question": "Can I still trade in my old SBMTD magnetic-stripe pass after the fare change?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nYes. MTD is offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused and partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass. [doc:sbmtd-farechange] Old passes will continue to work through August 31, 2025. [doc:sbmtd-farechange]\n\nTo see the exchange matrix for old to new passes, MTD's website has a button you can click, or contact MTD directly at 805.963.3366 (MTD Transit Center) or 805.963.3364 (MTD Business Office) for details on the specific exchange value of your pass.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-farechange"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Can I still trade in my old SBMTD magnetic-stripe pass after the fare change?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>Yes. MTD is offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused and partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass. [sbmtd-farechange] Old passes will continue to work through August 31, 2025. [sbmtd-farechange]</p><p>To see the exchange matrix for old to new passes, MTD's website has a button you can click, or contact MTD directly at 805.963.3366 (MTD Transit Center) or 805.963.3364 (MTD Business Office) for details on the specific exchange value of your pass.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fare Change</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["New fare structure for both cash & passes went into effect August 18, 2025. New passes became available on August 4, 2025. MTD will offer customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass. Old passes will continue to work through August 31, 2025. To see the exchange matrix for old to new passes, please click the button below.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses.\nBudget Scenario Operating costs have increased by more than 10% since FY 2019. The average fare revenue collected per passenger has dropped from $1.11 to $1.07, still rebounding from the COVID pandemic. Fare revenue only accounts for 20% of MTD’s annual operating budget. The inflation rate for the fiscal year ending June 30, 2024 was 3.2%, indicating a trend for additional increases.\nThe major revenue sources for the District operations are fares, Federal Transit Administration (FTA) operating assistance, and sales tax. With the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Federal operating assistance has grown a little; however, the apportionment of funds is fixed and there doesn’t appear to be much of a change looking forward. Sales tax revenues experienced growth with inflationary price increases during the COVID pandemic; however, recently these increases have been leveling out.\nRevenue shortfalls and increasing operating expenditures in the areas of wages and benefits, fuel, insurance, and utilities are expected, continuing to increase the deficit. Without a fare adjustment, the District would be forced to reduce service.", "Santa Barbara Metropolitan Transit District (MTD) is committed to providing reliable and affordable transit services to our community. While MTD’s fares have not changed in 16 years, cost pressures have come to the point of requiring a change in fare structure. We have carefully reviewed our fare system, received community feedback, and conducted a fare equity analysis. After careful consideration we adjusted our base fare from the previous fare of $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nImplementation of this new fare structure is a significant step toward assuring MTD’s services are dependable and economically sustainable in the long term.", "All fares are one-way and may be paid with coins, bills, passes, or via Tap2Ride with a contactless bank card or mobile wallet. Exact change required when paying with cash; fareboxes do not give change and drivers cannot make change.\nYou can take advantage of pay-as-you-go discounted daily and monthly pass rates when you Tap2Ride.\nClick here to learn more, including how to get a contactless bank card or to add a bank card to the mobile wallet on your smart device.\nStandard\n$2.50 Regular one-way Youth (K-12th grade)\n$1.25 Seniors (age 65+) Persons with Disabilities* Persons with Medicare Cards* (Cash or Tap2Ride **) *Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\nFREE Children under 45 inches tall (three children per adult) Transfers (to complete a one-way trip) For more information on Transfers, click here .\n$6.00 Day Pass (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$2.50 Token for one-way fare", "UCSB and SBCC Student Bus Pass Programs : All UCSB & SBCC students pay a transportation fee and may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.\nDiscounted Fare Programs: Individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth are all eligible for reduced fares. Discounts are also available for pre-paid passes including Day passes, 10 – Ride passes, and 30 – Day passes.\nFare Capping: Pay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet. You can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nFree Transfers: If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus. Transfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. Free transfers work as follows: upon request, riders can receive a free 60 minute transfer to be used to board a second bus, heading in the same direction (i.e., an outbound Line 20 to an outbound Line 6). Riders may also receive a transfer on that second bus that is good for 30 minutes, also headed in the same direction. You can find more information on free transfers here .\nOther Transfers: With a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!\nPacific Surfliner’s Transit Transfer Program: On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.\nAlternative Funding Sources: MTD is actively seeking new funding sources. MTD continues to apply to grant programs to operate and improve service, maintain and replace vehicles, and enhance transit facilities throughout the district."], "expected_facts": ["September 30, 2025"]}
{"id": "fresh-010", "question": "When was the last time SBMTD changed its fares before the 2025 change?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nThe last time SBMTD changed its fares before the 2025 change was January 1, 2009. [doc:sbmtd-farechange] The fares remained unchanged for 16 years until the new fare structure took effect on August 18, 2025. [doc:sbmtd-farechange]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-farechange"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>When was the last time SBMTD changed its fares before the 2025 change?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>The last time SBMTD changed its fares before the 2025 change was January 1, 2009. [sbmtd-farechange] The fares remained unchanged for 16 years until the new fare structure took effect on August 18, 2025. [sbmtd-farechange]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fare Change</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a 